      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>STORING NEGATIVES.</head>
        <p>STORING NEGATIVES.</p>
        <p>On the subject of storing finished negatives, "Lens," in the "Manchester City News," gives , a word of caution against using ordinary envelopes. If negatives are placed in a cool, dry place, either packed film to film in their original boxes or stored in proper negative envelopes, there should be no trouble with them. But he once put them in ordinary com- mercial envelopes, and one choice set of nega- tives after a damp summer developed a. yellow- ish St. Andrew's cross on every film, co.rrespand- ing to the bands of gum on the envelopes.</p>
        <p>PRINTS FROM LACE. Many things can be used for making, photo- graphic prints besides the orthodox negative. I have recently seen an album full cif ,contact prints which were made on platinum paper direct from lace. As the lace itself is white, the contact print St..pri,'S it as white on a dark ground, the " neE..tive" image in this case being the truer rendering. In other instances that can be named, such as prints from leaves, feathers, pressed towers, izc.;" this nega- tive image might not be so suitable. In such a case the contact print from the original can be made on p.0.p., printing very deeply and toning well. The print so made can then be used in its turn as a negative, arid ri positive print made from it, either on another piece of p.o.p. or in platinum, carbon, or arty other process that seems to be desirable.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MONUMENTS. The Bridge.—As we shall soon have the mists of Autumn on us, the amateur will have many chances of making a picture of the bridge whilst in the making. It will thus be a record photograph also, which the Secretary of the Warrington Photographic Society will be glad to add to the collection of historic and record photographs now being compiled by the society. It must not be thought that the giant arms of the cranes spoil a picture of this kind, al- though they can certainly be photographed in a way that does not improve it, as we have seen often. The softening light of a misty morning makes it well worth several attempts by an amateur at securing a picture of this great work in the constructing. It also tends to hide the ugly chimneys in the distance, objects that can never be made beautiful, try as we may. They, with telegraph poles, are an eye- sore of a transition age, where we have not yet learnt how to make the amassing of wealth any- thing but ugly. The big chimneys of Warrington are no exception, and are best hidden. I was told that the big shaft of Manningham Silk Mills at Bradford cost ten thousand pounds in the attempt to make it handsome; but no photographer, no painter has ever yet tried to put it in a picture. The camera placed at the Latchford end of Arpley footbridge would be pointed almost due north, with the light from the morning sun coming in on the right I hand. The beautiful double curve of the fin- ished portion makes a worthy foreground to the picture, and the whole combination of the handsome finished part, with the emblems of energy and industry beyond, is worth the spoiling of several plates, if a good one can be obtained. I have a negative of just such a subject in a similar atmosphere, hut without any bridge. It was taken in Cork Harbour, from the St. Patrick's Bridge, and is one of the few pictures I have taken that I am proud of. The work, too, at Warrington Bridge is rapidly</p>
        <p>approaching completion, and after a month or two the photographer's opportunities will be lost.</p>
        <p>SILVER-STREET.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
