      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>tettas to the Onitor</head>
        <p>tettas to the Onitor</p>
        <p>STREET TRAFFIC REGULATION.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l notice in your last issue that the "Old Journalist" deals with this subject in his con- tribution, and I was somewhat amused at the. logic of his case. Of course, he is quite open; he does not draw any conclusion, and I am al- most inclined to think that he is not tterious in his treatment of the subject.</p>
        <p>It appears to me that because an owner of one of these road hogs was successful in opposing a restriction in a certain old town that Warring- ton. must do absolutely nothing to prevent the preventable accidents which occur. I should very much like him to state a case for or against "better street regulation of traffic," which is so much needed, and, I be- lieve, so much desired, by a considerable num- ber of people in the town. I desire to draw his attention to the unique geographical position of Warrington, and also its industrial importance, consequent , upon which we obtain an increased motor and horse vehicular traffic. We have to contend not only with the internal traffic, which in itself would be quite sufficient (considering the narrow streets of the town), but we have to contend also with traffic from other towns and country dis- tricts, which is compelled to come through the town in order to reach its destination. Ido not think for a moment that anyone will deny the congestion, the danger therefrom, or the necessity of preventing accidents, but I am confident that opposition will come from the owners of the vehicles. Because of that I assume from your contributor " that we must sit still." The old traction engine was, and is now, com- pelled to have a flagman in front of it, but were I to suggest the same condition for motors, I should soon be told to consult a head special- ist. And yet I claim that it is more necessary that the motor should have one rather than the traction engine. Is it not a. fact that The owners of these motors are very seldom seen in the town on foot? They do more riding than walking, -so that it is very seldom that any of these men get knocked down by the road hogs. lam confi- dent that if " ," with a luxurious residence and plenty of wealth, were to get knocked down, the whole town would be up in arms, but is not the life of the worker a more valuable asset to the town than the residents of Belgravia with their motor cars? At any rate, the worker's life is as valuable to him as the life of any other individual, no matter what his rank, station or calling may be, and should receive adequate protection from the sharks of the road. I submit also that a given amount of wealth has been expended in order to educate and develop the worker, or child, as the case may be, and I think from that standpoint alone, i.e., the commercial, it is true to assert that the loss of a worker is a commercial loss. But apart from his commercial value he must be safeguarded as a pedestrian. Also those people who earn their livelihood by means of horse- drawn vehicles should have the lowest possible minimum of danger to contend with. In conclusion, let me ask "Old Journalist " : (1) Have the councillors power to impose a time limit? (2) Have they not the power to remove the time limit on Corporation motor 'buses? (3) Do the men in the Council Cham- ber look 'after the interests of the whole popu- lation or only a section? And, in the opinion of "Old Journalist," is it, or is it not, essential that thN question should be dealt with, or should we keep on having accidents, and then inquiries into them, which, in my opinion, is a waste of time if nothing is going to be done? GEO. CHAMBERLAIN.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>44 4..14014.1.11..1441.101.4014++++++++ + .1. .1. 44 POULTRY PEN $ .1. 4. .1. 4. Hints and Interesting Facts and 44 014 .1. .14 Chat for Amateurs. .1. q. ++.14.14.14401.4014.101..14.1•.1.44+4•401014 oi</head>
        <p>44 4..14014.1.11..1441.101.4014++++++++ + .1. .1. 44 POULTRY PEN $ .1. 4. .1. 4. Hints and Interesting Facts and 44 014 .1. .14 Chat for Amateurs. .1. q. ++.14.14.14401.4014.101..14.1•.1.44+4•401014 oi</p>
        <p>Why it should be that some breeds of fowls lay eggs the shells of which are deeply coloured, whilst others produce only pure white, and still more, eggs with shell lightly coloured, has never been explained. Many theories have been pro- pounded, no one of which is satisfactory. The one cogent fact is that all the non-sitting breeds lay white-shelled eggs, and it would appear to be true that as the maternal instinct weakens the colour of shell diminishes. On the other hand, however, we find that many of the sitting races lay white-shelled eggs. , It is of interest, therefore, to record that Professors Wood and Punnett have been engaged in endeavouring to accomplish this result at Cambridge, and so far with a fair amount of success. Whether that can be maintained remains to be seen, for what can be done in one or two seasons does not solve the problem. The qualities must be maintained in successive generations with increasing cer- tainty. An interview recently with Professor Wood showed that the birds he had worked with were black Minorcas and white Orpingtons, and that many of the chickens bred have been re- markably mixed in colour of plumage. The task is by no means easy, for if the non-laying- cum-brown-shelled combination is secured, it must be embodied in a fixed breed to be of real service. All poultry breeders will wait with deep interest further records.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>PEDIGREE LAYER.</head>
        <p>THE PEDIGREE LAYER.</p>
        <p>There is always a grave danger when buying new stock to have comparatively poor birds palmed off on one as pedigree layers, wonderful stories being told of how the birds themselves, or else their parents, produced 200 or more eggs the previous season. One of the difficulties of the whole question is that, as a rule, one has only the seller's word to go upon, and, while he may be speaking truthfully, it is quite possible he is not doing so, and the wonderful egg records his birds have made exist only in his own imagination. I heard of a case a few days ago, which, I think, our readers ought to know, since it may save them falling into a similar pitfall. A poultry-keeper anxious to improve the laying qualities of his flock, decided to buy a cockerel and three hens of a first-class laying strain. He bought the birds that were guaranteed to possess this ouality, and he was assured that their egg record during, the previous twelve months wag about 190 each. The birds arrived, looking re- markably poor, in indifferent health, and cer- tainly possessing none of the characteristics one usually associates with healthy and prolific fowls. During the following twelve months the three liens each averaged 62 eggs—somewhat different to the 190 that was guaranteed. The man from whom the hens were obtained made the excuse that the new conditions had ac- counted for this state of affairs; but this was so palpably a case of bluff that the purchaser was, naturally, far from satisfied, and he determined to follow the matter.up and thoroughly investi- gate it. This he did, and it was proved beyond the shadow of a doubt that the three hens came from a particularly poor laying strain, where practically no attention whatever had been de- voted to this feature. It was not a case woith fighting in the Courts, and so the matter dropped, but it shows how careful one must be when replenishing one's yard.</p>
      </div>
