      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>MOTOR NOTES.</head>
        <p>MOTOR NOTES.</p>
        <p>Why not a Society for he Prevention of Cruelty to Tyres? There is need of it. I draw my conclusion from observation of motor- ists in general; and in particular of two horse- owners who have lately beconte motorists. Every day I see drivers overloading, cuti;ing corners, leaving gaping cover cuts untreated, and braking with a savagery unbelievable. Then my ho flees; it is delightful to see witb what loving care they tend their horses' feet, cleaning frogs, washing heels, looking care- fully to every detail of the blacksmith's handi- work, blacking at polishing each hoof, in- sisting upon a dry clean stand in each stall. And it is disgusting to see these same men ignoring the appeals of their tyres for atten- tion. Both have under-tyred cars to begin with; both use the brake with positive ferocity, and neither even :thinks about cleaning his tyres or going ‘ever them for .surface cuts, or paying the least :attention to them—except to curse them soundly when .their burden of star- vation, negleet ,and utter Slavery becoming too heavy to bear, they ,give up the ghost. Would you half break a horse's law every , time you wished him to stop? !Nio. Then why slam or-the brake of your car and w -encn and strain your tyres? Would you :st a borse's feet .go =pared, or ,allow it to • olls!ct sharp stones? Then why not fill up the surface cuts</p>
        <p>in your covers after cleausing,the ,vo,lnds? Would you let your horse die a niist'be,-ause it was too much trouble to pump water? Then 'why slay your tyre by neglecting to pump suffi- cient air into it. Would you let a horse suff?r from "dry heel" for .months? Then why fail to Skidcreme the leather bands of your non- skids?</p>
        <p>The pneumaticide produces a huge crop of trouble, and in face of all that has been pub- lished On the treatment of tyres he cannot claim exoneration. Ignorance of the law is no excuse. One would think that every motorist In the land, without exception, would study the law of Tyre Care most assiduously and for the truly admirable purpose of guarding his own interests.: . Why not begin now, gentle reader? Save your pocket by having your covers repaired while . yet they can be repaired., Every cover taken in time is good ftr at least one re-tread. And note this well: the maker of the cover can best repair it, because he understands it. Examine your tyres at frequent intervals; fill their surface cuts; wash them after a wet, muddy run; test their air pressure at least once a week and inflate if necessary. Watch your rims for rust and roughness; keep them well painted. Discard security bolts; have the rim bolt-holes plugged and fit only tubes with bolt-valves. Fit tyres large enough to do the work the car demands of them; take corners easily; brake as gently as possible; start smoothly; have an eye to the parallelism of your front wheels; make sure that your brakes are evenly adjusted so that they do not throw nine-tenths of the strain on to one tyre. In the garage again—keep the floor free from oil and grease; at any rate where the cars stand, and where spares are stored. Keep spare tubes in waterproof bags or wipe them over with French Chalk, inflate very slightly (about two strokes of a hand pump will suffice), put inside spare covers and hang the covers up, away from light and heat as much as possible. All these things are so easy to do—and so profitable. If you have any doubt as to their simplicity, get a Michelin Guide, which in- cludes about 40 pages of useful information on the care of tyres. Do this—follow out the suggestions I have made above and I will guarantee that in three months' time you will have a lot less to say about " tyre expense"; for, believe me, it is riot nearly such a bogey when it is tackled in a reasonable way.</p>
        <p>BIBENDUAL</p>
      </div>
