      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>MAIZE AS A FODDER CROP.</p>
        <p>Professor It. G. White, of the North Wales Agricultural College, Bangor, writing of maize as a fodder crop, Bays: "The soil at this centre (Croesyokin, North Wales) was a strong loam on a clay subsoil, the previous crop being wheat. The seed was sown with a turnip drill in rows 26 inches apart during the first week in June. The land had received a heavy dressing of farm- yard manure, together with 4 cwt. superphos- phate and 4 cwt. kainit. The plot was troubled with birds at the start, but in spite of this it proved a magnificent crop, some of the plants being over 6ft. high. The horse-hoe was used three times and the hand-hoe once." Experi- ments were also made at Phis Major. The soil at this centre was a heavy loam, and was man- ured with farmyard manure, the previous crop being oats. "The maize was sown with a corn drill. The seed germinated very well and evenly, but the dry weather checked the subsequent growth, and the crop also suffered by the preva- lence of charlock on the plot. Had the weeds been kept down the crop would undoubtedly have been a success; as it was, it gave a good deal of useful fodder." These trials tend to show that under proper conditions maize- may prove a valuable CTOO in many districts of North , Wales, where a supply of succulent food is re- quired in early autumn. No damage was done by frost, though, as the crop was sown rather late at each centre, it is impossible to ,say that . there is no danger of iuch,damage in an average year. The trials are being continued, and the - success achieved so far north as the Bangor dis- trict of Wales may well encourage farmers in more genial English counties to try maize as a fodder crop. They must buy seed maize and not , use the common sort from a corn chandler's. Any seed merchant will supply the right type. , Mr. White sows 112 M. to the acre.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>CORKY SCAB ON POTATOES.</head>
        <p>CORKY SCAB ON POTATOES.</p>
        <p>The Potatoes (Corky Scab) Order of 1914 has just been issued by. the Board of Agriculture, and Sir Sydney Oliver, in his covering letter to local authorities, states: The Order, which does not come into farce till September Ist next, puts the disease known in England as Corky .Scab and in America as Powdery Scab on the list of plant diseases which it is compulsory to notify to the Board or the Local Authority by the occu- pier of any premises where po—tatoes affected with this diseae are grown. The diSease affects the surface of the tuber only except in very bad cases, and produces characteristic scabs which are liable to be confused with the common scab due to the mechanical action of the soil or to the presence of the organisms known as Oospora Scabies. True Corky Scab is believed to be very rare in England, and except in the mildest form to be confined to districts with a heavy rainfall, but the Government of the United States have prohibited the importation of potatoes from England, and thereby put a stop to what was at one time a large and important trade until the Board are able to satisfy them that this disease is unknown in the potato-growing districts, and that there is no fear that it will be introduced on consignments of potatoes sent to America. In order that the Board may be in a position to comply with the requirements of the American Government, I am to ask you to move your Local Authority to give wide publicity to this Order, and to render them such assistance in enforcing it as may be in their power. They do not think it necessary to ask your Local Authority to ap- point any officers or inspectors specially for this purpose, as they propose to instruct their in specters to deal with any case that may arise, but they would be glad if your Council would authorise any officer who may already have been instructed to deal with 'cases of Wart Disease of Potatoes to acquaint the Board should the pres- enoe of Corky Scab be brought to his notice. The Board propose also by the issue of posters and leaflets to explain the nature and appear- ance of this disease td Potato growers, and they will at a later date send you copies which they hope you will distribute, but in the meantime they will be glad if you will publish this Order in the manner best calculated to bring the matter to the attention of all persons concerned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>WORK OF THE MOMENT.</p>
        <p>Plant winter greens. If the late potatoes are wide in the rows put the plants in between. Also Plant colewort, cabbages and spring broccoli. Cure mildew on all garden plants and fruit trees by syringing with one ounce of sulphide of potassium in three gallons of water.</p>
        <p>Clip hedges and trees of yew, box, privet, holly, and thorn. Thin out weak shoots of dahlias and give liquid manure once a week.</p>
        <p>Prick out into nursery beds seedlings of peren- nials and biennials.</p>
        <p>TYING UP PLANTS.</p>
        <p>An important operation in the garden at the present time is the safe tying to stakes of plants that are at all inclined to wobble over. Many plants stand erect until the blooms develop, and then, becoming heavy with moisture, topple over, spoil the flowers, and disfigure the ap- pearance of the garden generally. Like every- thing else, there is a right and a wrong way of tying plants. The right way is to insert the stakes behind or between the foliage in such a way that they are not observable, and to make the plants appear in as natural a shape as pos- sible. Hollyhocks, although stout of limb, are apt to topple over. Drive a strong stake, three or four feet long, .securely into the ground, be- hind the plant, and tie loosely under a joint, but peke firm on the stake. Tall-growing bushy plants will require more than one stake if a natural, growth is to be maintained. For dahlias one good stake to each plant will be sufficient, securing the main stem as it advances. Chrysanthemums in particular require tying as growth proceeds, and one strong cane is generally , sufficient.</p>
        <p>THE IDEAL SWEET PEA.</p>
        <p>Size is the standard of excellence set up at our premier shows. For house decoration and gar- den display, perfume, quality and quantity is the standard. Which is it to be? asks the "Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Re- view." "Are we always to aim at growing blooms with stems over two feet in length and the thickness of a pencil, with cabbage-sized leaves, and with blooms distributed about half- way down the stem, as seen at our shows? Or shall we grow more naturally; on delicate stems, with well-arranged fragrant blooms at one end? Look at the question how we will, it resolves into one of coarseness versus refinement, mid it is to be sincerely hoped that the latter will win the day."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>RED HOT POKER PLANTS.</head>
        <p>RED HOT POKER PLANTS.</p>
        <p>The Kniphofia, to give it its botanical name, is a much admired plant when in bloom, some varieties looking like red hot pokers sticking out of the plants. The older forms bloom late in summer, but now there are varieties which com- mence flowering, in favourable seasons, in May, and go through till late autumn. It is neces- sary, however, to cut off the early flower stems. A new spike will spring from the crown. The colours of the new hybrids are rich and varied, and include yellow, salmon, and deep crimson'. The new plants vary in height, from two to five feet. •</p>
        <p>Seeds may be sown now in a good compost. Prick out when large enough and plant out in the autumn. Do not winter the roots in a heavy damp soil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>Fortnight's Camp in Cheshire.</head>
        <p>A Fortnight's Camp in Cheshire.</p>
        <p>ATTACKS AT NIGHT.</p>
        <p>To-day the members .of the Warrington Free Church Boys' Brigade strike camp at Acton. Bridge, where, for a fortnight, they have been dwelling in a canvas village. On the day the battalion proceeded into Che- shire a steam waggon and several carts were requisitioned for conveying camp necessities, whilst the. boys, aceompanied by 'drum and bugle, marched merrily on—now singing, now laughing—but always tramping with a hearty swing. On arriving at camp it was found that the fatigue party, under the command of Colonel 'Grounds and Adjutant and Captain Davies, had made full preparations, so that the boys were enabled to settle down without loss of time.</p>
        <p>NIGHT ATTACKS. Route marches have been undertaken to Winnington, Weaverham, Acton, and Delamere Forest, and ambulance work and signalling have been freely practised, the stations for the signalling being the Weaver Bridge and the top of the hill on which the camp is situated. Additional interest has been introduced into the camp life by the night attacks upon the en- campment. On August Bank Holiday night the attackers' plans were completely frustrated, but on the following night the tables were turned, for the defenders were surprised to find that the "enemy" bad broken through the guard and secured the flag. FRIENDLY BOY SCOUTS.</p>
        <p>There has been a complete understanding be- tween the boys' movements in the locality, and, as an evidence of this, ma Y be recorded a visit ' to the camp by the Acton Troop of Boy &amp;Puts, in charge of Scoutmaster Chetwynd. The visitors soon selected a tem to oppose the bri- gade in a friendly game of football—ending in a draw—and this was followed by the usual camp amenities. SPORTS AND CRICKET. Plenty of room for sPorts and bathing has been provided in the camp time table, and has been taken full advantage of. A number of the prizes were presented by residents of the district, to whom many thanks are due. The Bank Quay ("1)") Company gained the dis- tinction of obtaining the highest aggregate of marks in the sports, and, amongst their awards was a silver medal to Sergeant Roberts. The mile flat race was won by Private Grounds, of the Hill Cliffe (" C") Company, and he was given a silver medal. Members of the various companies have arranged cricket matches dur- ing their stay in camp, and during the wet evenings—two in number—singing and recita- tion competitions were the order.' A prize for the best solo was ,presented by Scoutmaster Chetwynd and won by Private Winstanley; this was in addition to several other prizes. THE RELIGIOUS SIDE OF THE WORK. Naturally the boys have been nurtured.spirit- ually as well as physically. The religious side of the work has received the Chaplains'—the Revs. J. S. Chedburn and H. Ogden—earnest attention. Every morning and evening prayers have been uttered around the flag staff,, and it was an impressive sight, after evening prayers, when the boys massed to salute the lowering of the flag. As an evidence of the Free Church unity, the services on Sunday were held in churches of different denominations. The morn- ing service was at the Wesleyan Church at Acton, when the preacher was the Rev. H. Ogden; whilst the Rev. J. S. Chedburn preached at the evening service at the Acton Bridge Baptist Church. A drum-head service was held on the camp ground on Sunday afternoon, when a large number of local residents gathered to share in the service and to hear the address by Mr. E. Gandy. It, ought to be added that the camp has been blessed by the unfailing kindness of Mr. Gandy and his immediate neighbours. HEALTH' OF THE CAMP. It is pleasing to report that the health of the boys on the whole has been excellent. One or two cases have had the kindly care and atten- tion of Dr. Smith, of Weaverham, who has manifestea his deep interest in the life of the camp by his almost daily attendance and gifts to the boys.</p>
        <p>, There are about 100 officers, N.C.o2's and boys in the camp; drawn from the following com- panies : Latchford Eaptist ("A") Company,. GralbOrne-street .("B") Company, Hill Cliffe (' C") Company, Wbrking Men's Mission ("-D") Company, and Earlestown ("E") Com- pany..</p>
        <p>is due to Colonel Grounds for the admirable fashion in which he has controlled and arranged the camp, and the heartiest of thanks are to be accorded to Mr. Nicholls, of Acton, who placed the camping ground at the disposal of the battalion. No account of the camp would be complete without an allusion to the general kindness that has been manifested towards the battalion generally by the inhabi- tants of the locality, quite a large number of whom gathered nightly to join in the •evening prayers, and to watch the displays by the boys.</p>
        <p>THE RETURN. • . The battalion will return by road late this afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>Wirth OkillS.</head>
        <p>Wirth OkillS.</p>
        <p>YCLIFFE P.S.A.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. on Sunday reference was made to the European. crisis, and the following resolution, unani- mously carried, was sent to the Foreign Secre- tary; the Prime Minister, and the local Mem- ber : " 'Phut we, the brethren of Wycliffe P.S.A., view with horror the terrible outrage to humanity and the menacing challenge to Chris- tianity involved in a European War. We desire to strengthen by every means in our power the strenuous efforts v hich England is making for limiting.the area .of conflict, main- taining our own peaceful relations with the nations of Europe, and preserving our freedom to act for peace as opportunity offew." The Chairman (Mr. B. Farrar, the secretary) made allusion to the international trouble, and Mr. Wright, giving the address upon "Man, what art thou?" spoke of the sympathy and unity that ought to exist between man and man, individuals and nations. Mr. C. Cotton and 31r. A. Healey also took part. in the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>RINGWORM ALL OVER HEAD.</head>
        <p>RINGWORM ALL OVER HEAD.</p>
        <p>16, Glover-street, Preston, Lanes., Eng.—" The ringworm began about a year ago with a small plake at the back of my head. I noticed the 'hair was leaving it. It gradually got worse and spread all over the back and sides of My head, leaving the places quite bare and also a small. place at the front. It was a very had disfigure_ merit and I suffered a great deal with ceaseless itching and burning. I could not sleep. " I was tired of trying things for the com- plaint, when I haiwened to read an advertise- ment about Cuticura Soap and Ointment, and decided to give them a trial. From the first I noticed what a clean appearance the affected places had after using the Cuticura Soap and Ointment and the pain was much easier. After a few weeks, first washing with. Cuticura Soap and then applying the Cuticura Ointment I received a cure and my hair began to grow again." (Signed) Syd Dark, Jan. 23, 1914. Al- though Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists throughout the world, a sample of each with 32-p. Skin Book will be sent post-free upon request. Address post-card: F. Newbery and Sons, ;.!7, Charterhouse-square, London, E.C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>Tradesmen Who Deliver Their</head>
        <p>Tradesmen Who Deliver Their</p>
        <p>Goods on Wheels.</p>
        <p>HEALTH-GIVING CYCLING.</p>
        <p>„. Money is certainly useful and the more we have of the currency of the realm the better we are pleased. The pace at which we live nowadays is much greater than it was even a decade ago, and we are all striving after,money and what it can do for us. Even so, but when we think about the question we agree that health comes before wealth. We live fast to- day, and there appears no prospect of any slackening of effort in any department of life: The greater necessity, therefore,-fa the rising generation is to possess a good constitution and high vitality, so that they will in the future be able to meet the strain of every-day existence. Judicious exercise plays an important part to this end, and certainly ode of the best forms that I know of is cycling. I say judicious exercise and not excessive. Cycling is admitted to be judicious; it is not of the strenuous or boisterous kind. Young people should be fitted with a suitable machine, and they should be controlled so that they will not over-work them- selves. Cycling has the advantage of. providing the rider with a variation of scenery, as well as supplying the lungs with pure air.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>Tarred Roads.</head>
        <p>Tarred Roads.</p>
        <p>. ..----. A sort time ago a lady who had a had slip on a newly-tarred road succeeded in obtaining heavy damages from a District Council. The danger is to be met with in many localities. It seems as though some road surveyors had no respect for the cycling section of the public, since the methods employed when tarring the road surface are far from beneficial to the rider. Their fault is that they neglect to cover the freshly-tarred surface with sand or gravel. If they only took into consideration the safety of the cyclist they would realise how greatly they are adding to the dangers of the road. Such a surface is very slippery, and even if a rider does succeed in negotiating it without an accident he may do so at the ex- pense of his clothes. Motorists suffer also in that it is difficult to remove the tar from the bodywork of their cars. Any cyclist-encounter- ing such a piece of road should report the mat- ter to one of the cycling, organisations, in order that the matter may be taken up with those responsible. Personally, I always dismount and walk—l consider my life more important than the few minutes which I waste by so doing.</p>
        <p>Cycling and the Public Weal. Two weeks ago I referred to a case which came to my notice in which a man had rigged up his knife grinding apparatus on his machine and I pointed out how this arrangement enabled him to cover a greater area of ground each day and to make his occupation more pro- fitable. I sometimes wonder if the majority of people realise how much they owe to the cycle in the direction of speeding up the trans- port of goods to the consumer's door. Every- one has noticed the number of butchers' and grocers' boys who deliver by cycle, and an interesting sight in some of our great cities— particularly London—is to see the enormous number of newspaper cyclists taking cpuires of their special journals at a fast rate to distri- bute to the newsboys at the various street cor- ners. They are expert riders, and, therefore, they shorten the time very considerably between the moment when the paper leaves the machine and is in the hands of the readers. I came across a ease the other day in which a newsboy cycled about 50 miles a day delivering his wares, lie was only a lad, but he has accom- plished this feat for many months. Working six days a week only, he rides 15,000 odd miles a year, and the public benefits by his' labour.</p>
        <p>The. Quality of Metal. A few years ago, if anyone had suggested that it was possible to obtain a piston speed of over a_ thousand feet a minute he would have been regarded as unpractical, to say the least of it. The motor engineer of to-day has designed and constructed ,engines that run at a much greater speed, and this without any 'deterioration, ex_ cent that which arises from ordinary wear and tear. There are few motorists who appre- ciate the reason for this accomplishment, yet it is a simple one. The necessary factor in this case is the quality of the metal employed. During the past few years the importance of the most suitable metal for each part of the has been recognised, but in the future still more attention will be paid to this point. The -question of construction played an impor- tant part in the early days, but as the design has become more and more -perfect the science of metals and their special uses has acquired an ever-growing importance. This is as one would expect. Design must proceed improve- ment in material. It is only by testing various parts that information- can be collected as to the, most suitable material. There can be no doubt but that the quality of the metal used in the motor car is far better than in any other branch . of engineering.</p>
        <p>The Taxation of Heavy Motors. About a month ago I referred to the occasion in which a deputation waited on the, President of the Local Government Board to urge the amendment of the law and regulations regard- ing heavy 'motor traffic. A very favourable reply was given by Mr. Samuel to that deputa- tion, as be stated then that he fully realised that the time had arrived When the whole question should be reconsidered. I see that Mr. Samuel gave voice to this opinion again the other day when speaking at conference. It appears from this that One result of the agita- tion that has been going on for so long has been to waken up those in authority and impress them with the importance of the matter. This subject concerns us as road users, ,and I be- lieve it will not be very long before motor 'buses, traction engines, trackless trolleys and motor lorries will be contributing a fair and just amount towards the cost of the mainten- ance of the roads. The ratepayer will then be relieved of a portion of his burden of taxation, or else will have better roads. An Interesting Legal Decision. Detachable wheels are now in such universal demand that the case recently settled by the ' House of tords possesses more than common interest. It was the final appeal from a deci- Sion carried through the Court of 'Appeal on the question of Whether the Riley detachable wheel was an infringement of-existing patents. The whole matter turned on the question of priority, and it was claimed •on behalf of the Riley that the patents in question had been anticipated. After a lengthy and very learned hearing their Lordships gave a unanimous decision in the Riley Company's favour, so that this'wheel, which has been on the market now for many years, is free of all other patent restrictions. We are likely to hear more of the Riley in the future, for the wheel is one of the most popular by reason Of its extreme simplicity and low price.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>LOCAL CLUB RUNS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL CLUB RUNS.</p>
        <p>" Cycling for Health" is the title of Bow den's new book. Or, rather, this is be title—the other half being "Points for CP: .lists." I hope next week to deal with latter half. The price of the book is a shillivg:, and it has been a vade mecum with us for InaIII years. The introduction by Mr.R. J.Mecre'll.; the famous editor of the "Irish Cyclist,'' really an introduction to the author, founder and present owner of the Raleigh CY`' Co., and is an inspiration in itself to anYY°llllll man desirous of rising in the world. " WilAee.; -physically, a broken down man, Mr. returned from the Far East, he had bougll'li tricycle and found that it improved his heali and broadened his mental horizon. - 'j. This led to his introduction into the cide :eft dustry and to the formation of the Eglei'd Cycle Co., now jointly owned by hitaseif 13;00 his only son, Mr. Harold Bowden, It i 9 largest concern of its class in the worlds esj ploying in the busy season over 1,700 PeoPle cer Producing annually ,about 60,000 bicycles, ‘/,,1 100,000 three-speed gears, and other speciAliti used in cycling." Here is an excerpt from Mr. Bowden'4 sot chapter on " Cycling for Health" long before I commenced cycling I had in a very feeble condition from a tropicai,,-011,r lish Colony, where my constitution had ently been irreparably injured by the del%,`lfii( I big climate, and two long periods of (liarrlicea, with all its attendant evils." ")-,,er evils he gives a list of—" inactive liver, lessness towards morning, bad circulliiels (causing cold feet), dyspepsia, constll°`l;eir Pains around the eyelids, varicose veinss,,,r. # inatisrn, hemorrhoids and general debillw;elit tendency to catch cold without any aPP3;s'er cause, and to perspire at the slightest `infl lion." It is a long and very interesting s.tots how, through the advice of a Harrogate di): o'f he was induced to take up cycling and n 5 jee° almost in the pink of health as maY from his photo in the frontispiece. A Btei:l3l like this