        <p>TWO CLUB RIDES: A CONTRAST' The Wesley-an Cycle Club rode in sPlet! weather to Castle Mill on Saturday-21 it was only when having tea at gra. V 750 worth's, Castle Hill, that the first few sl)°,001 rain came on. To give the weather a clinrl'7oo improving a short walk was indulged the woods, but no improvement. So downpour we were compelled to start Off, plus i;apes; some minus. A division effected, and the first batch got home o'clock. Troubles multiplied for thei; sections, for a burst occurred that wOO be righted, and everyone was drench' yof' before Warrington was reached. This tDe ever, only the fourth such experience six years' life of the club. 77d r 4 ,at" The other side was on Bank Beeston Castle. Twenty-eight rider through via Hatchmere, Tarvin, We° 11e Huxley. This gives the finest approach tad' celebrated ruin, and the magnificent, bolt!: castle-crowned, stands out with rage" srer e lent right until the foot is reached n . t tea was Served at the entrance gatespe' 1164 only drawback .being a charge of tilre:lol' each for storing bicycles in an reduced to twopence on account of th,c 'tt;The frie of the party. I will deal next week castle itself, under "Rides with an objec,ic'e t° have nothing like room to-day to do ins- this historic pleasure ground.</p>
        <p>CLUB RUNS TO-DAY. Warrington C.C.—Hunt's Criss 0)00° r.T.A.). Wesleyan C.C.—Middlewich. Sankey Bridges W.G.—Cuddington St. George's C.C.—Open run.</p>
