      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>ITTINGS BY AN OLD</head>
        <p>ITTINGS BY AN OLD</p>
        <p>JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>',ending Industry.</p>
        <p>!*l3day's Walton Park Show set me runiin- (yes, that is the word, cattle being in queg- M the general subject. Undoubtedly these ti exhibitions under the auspices of the Agricultural Society are of material bene- the farming interest; all the same, the mainly concerned are entitled to expict thtial and systematic aid and encourage- from the Board of Agriculture itself. The unentary Secretary to that Department Lucas) made a memorable announcement this important-point at the Conference Of nternational Association of Poultry In- ore; as all interested readers will remem- b was to the effect that Mr. Runciman was ring to overhaul and consolidate "the sYstem of agricultural education.' To 13' digress for a moment, I have long ht—and the success which, so far as my lbservation goes, invariably attends local Ments in that direction, certainly confirms IPression—that the more perplexing of the s involved would find their solution in Nuate scheme of State-aided co-operation. loteworthy remedy applied Of late years ted in the provision of agricultural col- by the County Councils where the rising farmers might acquire knowledge of the e and technique of their industry denied ir predecessors on the land; but these are em sufficient numerically and too many t farmers consider the fees prohibitive. It be borne in mind that the small farmers increasing class. As Mr. Sampson Morgan ided in the " Fortnightly " a while ago. rge old-fashioned farm is doomed, as far United Kingdom is concerned. The in- ng imports of foreign produce have reii- impossible the perpetuation of the system it represents.</p>
        <p>Not a Big Boom? t being the case, no wonder the Govern- And it impossible longer to neglect the of the English tenant farmers to reason- onsideration. The corresponding class in krk, Holland, France, Switzerland, and Ireland already enjoy abundant facilities LB are now indicated. Consider what their snt institutions have done for the farmers se countries and for the industry the world As Professor James Long remarked in one valuable contributions to the discussion, it the advantages which investigation and ary have conferred on agriculture during tat fifty years British farming must have ruined. Improved varieties of seed, the action of artificial manures, the self-bind- aPer, the mowing machine, the gas engine, 'earn separator and ingenious implements at variety, the methods of preventing crop ction by weeds and insect pests, the B of bacteriology—all have contributed to 138. The result is that, although prices aver and wages higher, we have better and larger crops of an improved charac- bile life upon the land is more popular sver. I repeat that such local shows as f Thursday last have played a really use- at in the forward movement here briefly 'ed; and assuredly, with an enlightened lost enterprising Liberal Minister at the the Board of Agriculture is destined ere ) supply that which our farmers as a body orely lack to enable them to compete on Lug like equal terms with foreign</p>
        <p>Yarns in Contrast. inch for serious argument; now for a para:; or two in- lighter vein. In the angling n I read that "it is more equal to all con- to fish in a canal (rather than a river), as e even current is obtained, and everyone .e. same depth of water to fish in." I was tied of two little stories of angling in very md very shallow water respectively. One la-Itish boatman hired by an angler. Said "Couldn't we try there, Larry— 'where's the ripple?" but the proposition kt once negatived with "No good, yer t.; dape wather." Questioned more par- as to this, Larry went on: "As dape as IleY—widout bottom, yer 'anner. Did yer itivver hear av the boatman of liillarney dape is this lake?' sez the gintleman he t. 'So dape there's no bottom to ut,' sez Nonsense,' sex the gintleman. sez the boatman, •me own brother fell av the boat last summer, an' sank like a 4 an' nivver came up ag'in at all." Pooh,' gintleman, that proves nothing." Well,' e boatman, all I know, sorr, is that the aa9 he telegraphed home from Australy B Sunday clothes.' " On the other hand. arter Platts solemnly asseverates that lie xlet a man who had been fishing the Ure 'ertain day during a particularly dry sum- then the river was extremely low. He lina one could easily imagine how little was coining down, because just after he "aght his second fish the stream stopped IY, and he could not for the life of him at for the phenomenon until he suddenly 'ered that two cows were both drinking at rae time about a hundred yards above him 1* Eating Extraordinary.</p>
        <p>ews item in last week's "Examiner" re- that the Marine Department of the Zealand Government is doing for the ' fisheries there what our own farmers .0 the British Board of Agriculture to do !..e home .industry. It somehow set me ing of an oyster-eating feat of which I in this column a while ago, and of other uloraic enterprises of the kind concerning I have accumulated evidence in the mean- Without any attempt at chronological • let me barely mention a few others who achieved a questionable fame by their 1'3116 and insatiable appetites for what the reporter delights to describe as the " sue- t bivalve." There was, 'of course, the im- Li panda. Then there was the famous ✓ of Colchester who, a poet, being the de- kt, is said at least " sometimes " to have half his own weight of oysters in a day." letter to the "Times" full 40 years ago. Backland made a remarkable reference Subject. He was discussing the oysters Taries on the north sides of the mouth of names. These, he said, have green beards. cause of this greenness is certainly not but the food upon which they subsist in IPartieular locality. That they are not i re is proved by the fact of one cockney eating not long ago 370 of these • a sitting without injurious conse- 4.''' • Again there was Sieur Laperte. who. delag to Brillat-Savarin, disposed of thirty- Without discomposure. This fmt • s Ivl d he began his dinner, and (says • a Ig arin) "behaved like a starving man. tt'anden Review" of the latter end of 1800 he following as having occurred on the Ei'teDteraber in that year: "A young lady at On, daughter of a respectable family ill „le Undertook to eat for supper the enor- ortio"titY of three hundred oysters. with a Etsto 11. Of bread, which she performed. to wn_ls.liment of all present." • Beside this re hid. the German Princess who married COnchLOUS XlV.'s brother boasted in a nik el aunt Sophia of Hanover, pales into • tabe., lice. Her Highness wrote: "We rose 441117 half an hour ago. Monsieur. Mme. oyst " and I ate nearly two hundred Eng- ele between us. For my part I have 50 zhe • 'Lone. de Chartres the aquae number, usien r must have eaten eighty."</p>
        <p>cAN'T BEAR TO HEAR YOUR CHILD CRY. Keep his stomach I:3l'cler and you will soon find his ReZPer improved. Next time he irritable, try a Steedman's Nothing else is just as Obsorvc; EZi tecdmall..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>TABLE TALK,</head>
        <p>TABLE TALK,</p>
        <p>The World's Tribute of Interesting</p>
        <p>Items of News.</p>
        <p>QUAINT AND AMUSING.</p>
        <p>Steamship men in New York say that the number of bicycles taken aboard by Americans for use on touring trips in recent year ti is so small as to be negligible. Time was (says the " Westminster Gazette") when a different Con- dition existed, when the bicycles and tricycles and tandems which annually went over-sea in the holds of ocean liners were numbered by thousands. The company which wished to make the greatest appeal for patronage was careful to afford every facility for the care and handling of the vehicles. These were the days before the advent of the motor car.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>Left Boot First.</head>
        <p>Left Boot First.</p>
        <p>If you were to inquire of the ordinary man in the Street on which foot he wore out the sole of his boot or shoe the sooner (says the "Daily Mirror"), he probably could not tell you, hut if you were to ask him to make sure of noticing next time and tq let you know you would find it is nearly always the left foot. • I know this for a fact, because I have tried it on a number of my acquaintances, and it has nearly always proved correct. Never, though, till the other day did I hear an explanation advanced. A journalist friend attributes the phenomenon entirely to stepping off omnibuses and tram- way cars While in motion "left foot first."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>Atlantic Fog Tests.</head>
        <p>Atlantic Fog Tests.</p>
        <p>Some result; of much interest to navigators are likely to be obtained from the tests con- ducted on the Canadian Northern R.M.S. Royal George, on a voyage from Avonmouth to Que- bec and Montreal. The liner was fitted with the Marconi Bellini Tosi apparatus, which is designed to enable a ship in the thickest wea- ther, and without the aid of the compass or sextant, to find her position when she is within a radius of fifty miles of a fixed wireless station, or one on board a ship whose position is known. Also, should she pick up the wireless distress signal on this apparatus, she will be able to set her course towards its source even though the ship in distress has not been able to give her position. This is the first time the equipment has been carried on an Atlantic liner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>Bear's Curiosity.</head>
        <p>A Bear's Curiosity.</p>
        <p>Sam and Barbara, the polar bears at the Zoo, are having great times at present. Barbara was (the "Spectator" records) greatly inter- ested in the way the bath was filled. The water comes in with a fine spurt from a hole in the concrete, and she got 'under the stream and let the water play all over head and shoulders, opened her mouth wide and let it splash in over her throat and tongue, kicked over back- wards and came up for another douche over her .ears and neck. Then she tried to find-out how the water .came in, thrust her Paw as far as she could into the hole; and nearly stopped the water altogether. Sam came up to investigate; she rolled On him, bit him, stood on her head, and came up again to receive the stream full in her mouth, whence she turned it meditatively into the bath.</p>
        <p>Europe's Best Big Game Preserve. Wolves are increasing very considerably in numbers in certain parts of Lapland, and last winter some of them were actually seen within ten or twelve miles of Ortersund, Jamptland. During the last twelve years, according to the official figures, 124 bears, 324 wolves, 175 lynxes, 992 gluttons, 253,406 foxes, 50,822 seals, 175,975 eagles, eagle owls, falcons and hawks, and 2,400,000 grey crows have been killed in Sweden. In Norway last year 27 bears, 31 wolves, 38 lynxes, 37 gluttons, 10,325 foxes, 1,510 martens, and 1,266 otters were killed, as against 16 bears, 15 wolves, 35 lynxes, 36 gluttons, 11,922 foxes, 1,614 martens, and 1,454 otters in 1912.—" L," in "Country Life."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>Gold in Ontario.</head>
        <p>Gold in Ontario.</p>
        <p>An Ontario prospector rec'ently arrived at Fort William from the northern part of that province with 43 samples of gold which he had discovered north of Duck Lake, about 150 miles north-east of Fort William. The gold samples secured are embedded in rock, some of which have been analysed and show a very high per- centage of the precious metal. If the ore exists in any quantity, which remains to be deter- mined, it will, according to these. specimens, prove to be a valuable find. It is 'stated that large offers have already been made to pros- pectors for their claims.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>" Pelorus Jack's" Disappearance.</head>
        <p>" Pelorus Jack's" Disappearance.</p>
        <p>. _ Further confirmation of the disappearance from the French Pass in New Zealand of the strange white fish known as "Pelorous Jack" was given -by Dr. M'Nali, the Australasian his- torian, who arrived in Sydney recently. Dr. At'Nali said he had as a fellow-passenger Cap- tain Lambert, of the Union Steamship Com- pany's steamer " Arahura," a vessel which made trips several times a week through the pass. Captain Lambert has probably seen " l'elorus Jack" more often than any other living man, and he has not seen the fish for more than a year:-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Flowers in the Arctic.</head>
        <p>Flowers in the Arctic.</p>
        <p>The Arctic region is not bare and lifeless. Mr. Aubrey Fullerton, in an article in the cur- rent "Chamber's Journal," tells that flowers there are legion. The view of the topmost country, as seen while coasting along the Arctic shore in July and August, is normally a view of long grassy slopes profusely abloom. A bl\le lupin, a minisuterrhododendron in a mass of red blossom, and a little white heather, whose stalks make serviceable fuel, are typical of the Arctic flora. There are even blueberries 70 miles up the Coppermine. On the Herschel' Island, off the northernmost coast of the Yukon Territory, there is a floral pageantry every sum- mer. It includes the wild rose, which grows nowhere else so far north. Blue and white daisies, forget-me-note, bluebells, the dainty aconite, and marguerites, all of them small and wonderfully delicate, and banked in moss, spring abundantly from the otherwise barren soil. Their beauty is short-lived, but im- mensely cheering to the roughened sailor a and whalers who come round from Behring Sea to Herschel' every year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>"The Divine -Plant."</head>
        <p>"The Divine -Plant."</p>
        <p>Coca, from whose leaves cocaine is produced, was known among the Incas as the "divine plant" long before the Western world was thought of in the East, and it played an inter- esting part in their lives. The young men of the tribes engaged in athletic contests, and among the most coveted rewards the victors could expect were little pouch6s filled with cer- tain dry leaves. Accompanying the pouch of leaves, or chuspa, as they called it, was a small gourd known as the popora, containing lime; the leaves had been carefully plucked from the "divine plant," NOVI(' the lime served to make them more pleasing to the taste. The two sub- stances combined formed a strength-producing element of wonderful power. Subjects of the Incas who thus fortified themselves by chewigig coca leayes believed their bodies posses4ed super-liumai cudurauce,</p>
        <p>the last four centuries? Have you mastered the known facts in electricity? What are you waiting for? A better organisa- tion of society? Will that make the public library any more hospitable and soul-Satisfying than it is to-day? Obtaining the ballot for women and children? Will that start you studying the noble discoveries in natural science, already at your disposal, so numerous pow that the remainder qf. your life will barely be time enough for opening them up?—" The Woman's Magazine."</p>
