      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>Literary Gleanings.</head>
        <p>Literary Gleanings.</p>
        <p>"TINNED WHITE MAN."</p>
        <p>There is an amusing story told by the Bishop of Yukon, who has been on a visit to this coun- try.</p>
        <p>He once made a long journey to visit a remote tribe of Indians, hardly any of whom had ~ever: seen a, white Man before. One of the members of the expedition took with him a gramophone, thinking it would interest the Indians. .</p>
        <p>It did! When it was set going they all gath- ered round it in wonder. • They were rather frightened, but when the record was finished they crowded up to make a closer inspection. Finally, an old chief, getting down on his knees and peering into the trumpet whence the mysterious voice had come, muttered, "Where him come from?"</p>
        <p>"Far, far away," was the reply; "from a place called Chicago." "rgh !" grunted the chief. "Tinned white man,"--- Boys' Own Paper."•</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>"HAPPINESS" FALLACY.</head>
        <p>THE "HAPPINESS" FALLACY.</p>
        <p>In most of our large cities there is a library with the best thought of mankind in accessible form, with no cost in money to the reader. Free education is open to everyone in school. Uni- versal opportunity is already here. If only the lazy, who think that life ought to present them with a happy fate, could see that their best destiny lies in hard work! They fill their even- ings, and even their nights, with reckless time- consuming pleasures. Every holiday is a period taken for more of the same- drugging. If life is narrow and difficult, the way out is to change it by enriching capacity and strengthening powers. Is there no department of science that appeals? Have you read all the great English poetry of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>ENGLAND AN ISLAND NEAR JERSEY.</head>
        <p>ENGLAND AN ISLAND NEAR JERSEY.</p>
        <p>"The Sunday at Home" contains an article by Marie d'Eglantine on "Holidays in France," which gives interesting little peeps of French domestic life. In the—course of the article -the I writer says : . " The fact that our home was across the sea in England opened many hearts to us in our wan- derings about France. I remember once, when I cycling, taking shelter in a farmhouse on the I borders of Normandy and Brittany, where there lived three old people, a brother and two sisters. I cannot guess at their age, for tire country people in France age quickly, but neither of the sisters had left the neighbourhood of the farm for fifty years, and the brother, who went to the nearest town, perhaps ten miles away, to the market, was considered a great personage because he had seen a train! In the twentieth century! . . .</p>
        <p>"Our arrival on bicycles was the event of the year. The road was quite off the beaten track, and they had never seen a woman on wheels before. Their curiosity was intense. " Is London the other aide of Paris?' one of the women asked, and hearing it was in a coun- try called England, which you reached by boat, they appealed incredulously to the old man. " Yes, yes,' he said with a wise expression,</p>
        <p>for you iu Europe; please do your bzpt fo: By</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>' England is an island near Jersey. I have seen the advertisements of the boats that go there.' "</head>
        <p>' England is an island near Jersey. I have seen the advertisements of the boats that go there.' "</p>
        <p>A HAPPY SEQUEL TO A HOAX..</p>
        <p>A story which, if not true, is picturesquely invented, is told in the smoking-room of the House of Commons as to how Lord Granard be- came a member of • his Majesty's Government. A_ captain of the Scots: Guards, he held the unique position of being the only member of the officers' mess professing Liberal principles. This was frequently made the occasion of good- natured badinage. One day in 1906, whilst , Campbell-Bannerman was in town engaged upon the task of forming his Government, Lord Granard received a note from the Premier in- viting him to call at Belgrave-square. Hasten- ing to obey the summons, he was duly admitted to the presence of the Cabinet-maker. He was surprised and finally embarrassed by Campbell- 13annerman's evident reluctance to come to the point. He started various topics of conversation and did not fail to observe that the over-worked Premier was growing impatient at the length of his visit. At last Campbell-Bannerman, half- rising, banteringly asked whether he could do anything for his morning caller. Fortunately Lord Granard had brought with him his sum- mons, which was promptly denounced as a hoax. " But as you are here," Campbell-Bannerman continued, "I should feel it a great advantage if you would accept the post of Lord-in-Waiting, which would give you the opportunity of conduct- ing in the House of Lords some departmental business." The offer was promptly accepted. Lord Granard decidedly had the best of the joke, since Ile obtained honourable ministerial office, and a salary of £702 a year, a sum considerably in excess of a captain's pay.—From "Sixty Years in the Wilderness," by Sir Henry Lucy, in the " Cornhill Magazine" for August.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>BI4TANNIA</head>
        <p>BI4TANNIA</p>
        <p>iep doing my i•(;.1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <p>Irietors of "Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <p>My Dear Children,— One scarcely knows what to say this week, as we have lived in stirring times. As every- one is too much involved I do not think I should trouble you with a lengthy letter. Next week I hope matters will have settled down, and we can resume our usual weekly chat. Your loving</p>
        <p>UNCLE JACK.</p>
        <p>MY FAVOURITE FLOWER. The lovely rose of summer, The stately mignonette, The snowdrop in the springtime, And modest violet Are only substitutes to me, For 'neath the greenwood tree Is the daffodil nodding in the wind, The sweetest flower for me.</p>
        <p>The lily of the valley, The primrose in the wood, The daisy in the meadow, Are made by God, so good; But these are only substitutes, For 'neath the greenwood tree Is the daffodil nodding in the wind,. The sweetest flower for me.</p>
        <p>I love to sit where bluebells grow, And the iris, queen of all, But she looks at flowers-below her Which are pretty, but very small; But these are only substitutes, For 'Heath the greenwood tree Is the daffodil nodding in the wind, The sweetest flower for me.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>SECRET CAVE.</head>
        <p>THE SECRET CAVE.</p>
        <p>Milly Penrose and her brother Bob lived with their grandmother and their Uncle Jack in an old rambling house, which had been built in the time of Queen Elizabeth. The house was about two miles from a little village in Cornwall, and stood upon a cliff above the sea, very lonely. Milly was twelve years old, and Bob was ten. Their parents were in India, and they lived with their grandmother. Milly and Bob had been told by the butler of a secret staircase on the other side of one of the oak panels in the library. This staircase had three hundred steps, and led down to a secret cave, which was one of a series of caves called the " Smugglers' Caves." Milly and Bob had often tried to find the secret panel, but as the library was a very, large room they had not succeeded.</p>
        <p>One afternoon in the middle of October, Uncle Jack had gone to a large town on busi- ness, and Grannie had gone to visit an old friend who lived some ten miles away, and was not expected home till dark. Milly and Bob had their friends in to tea with them—Joyce Green, who was thirteen years old, and Ronald Smith, who was ten years old. Joyce and Ronald were cousins, and Ronald lived with Joyce because his parents were dead. The children could not go out, as it was raining, and Milly suggested playing hide-and- seek, which was agreed upon. After a time, however, the children became tired of it, and Joyce said: "Oh, what can we do now? I do wish it would stop raining." "I know," said Bob. "Let us try and find the secret panel in the library."</p>
        <p>- This was hailed with delight, and with one accord the children matched off to the library and began the search. After a few minutes the panel beside which Joyce was standing suddenly slid open, and they all stood spellbound. None of them knew just how the panel had opened. The best thing to_do was to try and find out—they did so.</p>
        <p>Soon Bob caught sight of a small knob near the floor; he touched it and the door closed; he touched it again and it opened. "I reckon you must have knocked it with your foot, Joyce," he said, and then added, "Let's explore, you chaps." "Oh, yes," shouted the others.</p>
        <p>Bob then ran to the stables and brought back two lanterns, which he lit, handing one to Ronald, the other he took himself.</p>
        <p>Bob led the way, Joyce following, then /dilly, Ronald bringing up the rear. The staircase was very narrow, and, as it was quite dark, except for the lanterns, the children had to go down very slowly and carefully. At last they found themselves in a cave, in 'which there was an old table, three empty wooden boxes, two wooden chairs, and on a ledge of rock jutting out there were • three spoons, two knives, three forks, four tin plates, ' four tin mugs, and a frying pan. "I say," said Milly, "there must be some way out of this horrid place." As she said the words Bob noticed, by the help of the lantern, a kind of trap-door arrange- ment cut out of the roof of the cave, which with a great deal of pushing they managed to dislodge. With the help of a chair, Bob climbed ; through, the others following. They then dis- covered a cave in which they had often been before, as it was one of the smugglers' caves, which tourists generally came to see. "Nothing doing here," said Ronald. "1 vote we go down to the secret cave again." They did so.</p>
        <p>Once more in the secret cave, Ronald found a low opening, which led into another cave, in which the explorers discovered six boxes and a great assortment of tools, hammers, files, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>"Shall we try and open them?" said Joyce. • "Yes, I think so," said Bob. "It wouldn't he such a dusty plan, would it, Ronald, old boy?" "Not half," said Ronald. •</p>
        <p>And so they commenced opening them. In the first and second boxes they found some very stale tobacco, which had a horrible smell. The third, fourth, and fifth were filled with gold and silver coins. The sixth box was lar- ger than the others, and more difficult to open. When it was opened the first thing they saw was a small iron box. Joyce opened it. In the box there was a great assortment of necklaces, bracelets, rings studded with jewels, and also odd jewels lying—diamonds, rubies, emeralds, sapphires, and opals. Then were discovered underneath the small box gold and silver ! candlesticks, gold drinking cups, and many silver dishes. "Let's put them back, and then fetch some of the men to carry them up for us," said Joyce. "Right oh," said Bob, and Ronald and Milly started off, while Joyce and he Waited. After what seemed an hour to the two in the cave, footsteps were heard, and the other two returned with three men. Four of the boxes were carried up, the two containing tobacco being left. The party had just reached the top of the staircase when Uncle Jack walked into the library, having caught an earlier train than usual. - _</p>
        <p>"Hullo, there, what's all the noise about?" he cried, on seeing the children.</p>
        <p>" Ah, 'You just wait and see, Uncle Jack," said</p>
        <p>All the servants came into the library, and everyone was very excited. Grannie came in in the middle of it all, and even she was ex- cited.</p>
        <p>The children felt very proud and happy when, a week later, Uncle Jack took all four of them to a town, where the treasure had been put in a museum.</p>
