        <p>Power made in the very act of asking us to join it in breaking treaty obliga- tions to another nation. And so the Government have their consciences and all parties in the State behind them. We shall present a united front to the enemy such as our annals have ' rarely witnessed. When madmen are let loose upon humanity, humanity, as a body, must look to itself. No effort will be spared to crush this monster, this human butcher, this anachronism of the Twentieth Century, and when it has been crushed we hope, and believe, the German people will take the control of affairs in their own hands and so provide that for the future they and we shall be free from the fear and sin of such criminal upheavals.</p>
        <p>THE CONDITIONS OF NEUTRALITY. SIR EDWARD GREY'S speech was a review of British foreign policy, as far as concerned this country s obligations to France, from the time when he undertook the responsibilities of the Foreign Office. It was followed with the profoundest attention, but the pas- sages on which the House hung with breathless eagerness were those which told the conditions on which SIR EDWARD GREY believed that we could remain, and those on which we could not remain, outside the area of conflict. To be able to make certain of remaining outside, we must proclaim our uncon- ditional neutrality: That we could not do. The conditions under which, we could remain neutral were :</p>
        <p>(1) That Germany should respect the neutrality of Belgium; (2) That Germany should abstain from attacking the north and west coasts of France, and French ship-</p>
        <p>ping. Prance and ,Germany had both been asked to give an assurance that they would respect Belgian!. neutrality. France had given it; Germany refused on the ground that an answer might disclose their plan of campaign. On the previous day, he said, he had told the French Ambassador :</p>
        <p>" I am authorised lo give an assur- ance that if the German Fleet comes into the Channel through the North Sea to undertake hostile operations against the French coasts or shipping, the British Fleet will give all the pro- tection in its power." This assurance is of the greatest impor- tance, but it is not the obligation which the Government feels to support France under the specified contingencies that has been the ultimate cause of war. War has been precipitated by Ger- many's violation of the neutrality of Belgium. SIR EDWARD GREY made it clear that in honour and to safeguard our own interests, the two conditions under which we could remain neural had to be observed. Our obligations to France were vital. To look after our interests in the Mediterranean the French Fleet had been drawn from their own North and West Coasts, and we had undertaken to guard them. The cunning of the German militarist party was displayed by their callous reply. They were, they said, willing •to foregO their right to bombard the French coasts under England's protec- tion ; for which we might say, "Thank You; for nothing." But they proposed to strike at France on the flank by vio- lating Belgian territory. They adhered to this and so brought Belgium and England in the balance against them.</p>
        <p>' ' _ ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>By kARJORIE.</head>
        <p>By kARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Queen Alexandra has spent six consecutive months of this year in London, to the great gratification of the citizens and dwellers in the Metropolis. She has also appeared more fre- quently in public this season than for four years past, and it is hoped that she may emerge from her retirement in a still greater degree during the winter and next year. Queen Alexandra, as everyone knows, is interested in everything that tends to assist the improvement and betterment of the working and poorer classes. But it may not be generally known that Her Majesty looks over every appeal that reaches her—and these are thousands in a year—and spends many honrs</p>
        <p>„ in dictating replies. Everyone seems glad to take up any charitable work suggested or in- spired by Queen Alexandra, who has the gift of imparting her enthusiasm to those whom she interests in her schemes of charity, or indeed any work in the cause of humanity. War and Health Resorts.</p>
        <p>Notwithstanding the Continental war and political crisis, London is now supposed to be "empty," which, however, only means that some hundreds of thousands of its six million inhabitants have distributed themselves over the various summer holiday resorts. Among the few who stand to gain from the fear of a general European conflagration are those with interests in our British and Irish spas and health resorts. Apprehension will keep the more wealthy class of holiday makers at home. Those who were contemplating visiting the Tyrol, Marienbad, or the Bohemian spas, will think it better to change their plans, and there will be a general disposition to avoid crossing the seas. American visitors on the Continent will also be scared, and although some may hurry back across the Atlan- tic, it is probable that a large number will tarry in this country longer than their original ar- rangements contemplated. It therefore seems likely that all the home resorts, which cater for the most wealthy community of holiday-makers, may expect a record influx of visitors.</p>
        <p>Two Titled Hotel-keepers. There are aristocratic hotel-keepers, as well as milliners and dressmakers. Lady Burton and Lady Augusta Inskip, nee Orr-Ewing, both run very superior hotels and most successfully. The hotel at Avimore, situated on the Scottish pro- perty of Baroness Btirton's husband, Mr. Baillie, was built, furnished, and decorated under the personal supervision of her ladyship. It is , more like a comfortable country house than a hotel, and is much appreciated by tourists. The hotel of Lady Augusta Inskip at Portpatrick was built by the late Mr. C. Orr-Ewing, M.P., who furnished it luxuriously, even going so far as to place valuable old pictures on the walls of several rooms. Travellers by the steamships from Portpatrick to Ireland are agreeably sur- prised to find so comfortable a hostelry in this unfrequented corner of Scotland. The Frenchwoman in the Home. The social and economic position of French- women and Eritish and Irish women has little in common. Woman has always been the most important factor in French family life. Even as a girl at home, much deference is shown by father and brothers to the judgment, or rather to the intuition of la jeune fille, and since the Revolution woman has been a factor to be reck- oned with in politics. The wife pleases and in- fluences her husband as a friend, and her en- deavour is to make all about her happy and to create an atmosphere of charm, which follows and sways her menfolk at home and abroad. She rules the home, and her fortune plays as import- ant a part economically as she does socially. There are no housekeeping allowances in France, and in the lower middle classes the woman holds the purse, because her husband willingly admits that she is more competent than he is to dis- burse the contents. The majority of Frenchmen are not ashamed to admit that women are the backbone of the nation.</p>
        <p>Tunics and Bodices. There is little doubt that the long tunic has come to stay, and if choosing a gown that is to be worn for any length of time, it would be wise to have it made in this style. It must, how- ever, be borne in mind that the box pleats at the back are quite unsuitable for people with wide hips, and the same may be said of the pleated tunic in any form. The newest design has a quite plain bodice, which is at the same time loose fitting, the fulness being gathered slightly in the centre. It fastens in front, with buttons placed closely together. The underskirt, which is more like a petticoat, as far as import- ance is concerned, is quite plain and very skimped. It is also of plain material to match the bodice, whilst the tunic is striped and pleatkd, and reaches to within about eight inches of the feet. Sometimes the bodice is pleated to match the tunic which is often cut with a point in front. In this case the pleats of the corsage are fastened into Raglan sleeves. The waist- band is rather wide, and the whole fastens in front. With medici collars and cuffs, the tunic of this description look's more like a coat than a dress.</p>
        <p>The Yoked Tunic. , Tunics with deep yokes, which cover the hips entirely, are to be recommended for those who are no longer slim. The pleating begins below the hips, and the tunic reaches almost to the feet. This style is very becoming to the tall woman. Braces starting from the waist pass over the shoulder, and back of the corsage, which is of a different material, matching the skirt. The yoke buttons in front. There is a decided change in the silhouette of the moment. Coats are much fuller in the skirts, and tunic adds considerably to the width of The the pleated figure. The fuller skirt is bound to collno:,:ed by scanty dresses of the Empire crinoline, and to go still further ebraeckf:flloizraabnetehx,s- ample, the farthingale of Queenl'mmodes of the reign succeeded to the severely previous century. There isi.enodsf I rye of the ultra line in these times, r of the crino- line swings from of fashion always narrow skirt are numbered amndontheeexpternedmuelutmo another.</p>
        <p>Liverpool's Policewoman. That Liverpool should have one policewoman . and is asking for more, whilst London has none, would seem to infer that the Metropolis is be- hind the great Lancashire seaport in this respect. As a matter of fact, women have for some time rendered valuable and constant aid to the authorities of Scotland Yard, although not occupying any official status. At police sta- tions, women, when arrested, are put under the charge of members of their own sex, who care for them and supply their wants, whereas some years ago these offices were performed by men only.</p>
