      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, AUGUST 8, 1914.</p>
        <p>, '</p>
        <p>H</p>
        <p>ili rl</p>
        <p>'</p>
        <p>W</p>
        <p>FOR LIBERTY AND HONOUR. A week ago there were many in this land who thought that a position of British neutrality in the European War would best accord with our interests and the ultimate good of humanity.; to-day there is not a nran or woman in the whole Empire but will vehemently declare for war to the bitter end against that man of blood, the GERMAN EMPEROR, and the system under which he is allowed to breed despotism and plot the murder of innocent peoples. Let it be clearly understood that we have no quarrel with the people of Germany. They are, we know, a pro- gressive, scientific race; they are, we believe, in the bulk, a peace-loving. people. We had hoped to have them as colleagues in the march of civilisa- tion towards that goal, the attainment of which all sane men desire—the time when the game of war shall have suc- cumbed to the arts of peace. But we are to be estranged by the mad act of an insane ruler—so mad that, as Mr. BONAR LAW finely put it, he is attempt- ing to play Napoleonism upon Europa, without a Napoleon. We, fear greatly that he has sold his people ; he tried to buy us, but he failed, as lie was bound to fail. Anyone not blindedty a self- adoring spirit such as the world his rarely known could have seen that his schemes were bound to fail ; at any rate, that scheme would not have been so arrogantly and shamelessly put forth for all decent men to scoff at by anyone in his senses. SIR EDWARD GREY must have been in a trying position. Being in possession of the most unscrupulous correspond- ence that the histories of the nations can show, he was pressed hard on the side of peace—a peace he knew he dare riot countenance. The White Paper, published this week, not only justifie;• his actions, step by step, but proves that he would have been utterly un- worthy to call himself a British states- man had he not stood firm. Our regard for the honour and the fair fame of ' this country ranks before our fear of war, and its horrible possibilities ; but there is the question of national. safety involved here as well. When Mr. ASQUITH asked for supplies of men and money he was able to say that we were going into the fight with a clear con- science. He reviewed the events which led up to our declaration of war against Germany, and summarised the pro- posals made to us as Germany's infamous proposal." That proposal, he said, with a fire that is seldom seen in the Commons, would have meant that behind the back of Prance we should havp given free licence to Ger- many to annex the whole of the French colonies, and that we should have bartered away to the Power that was threatening Belgium our obligation to keep our plighted word. Britain had been forced into the war, and was fighting to fulfil a solemn international obligation and to vindicate the principle that small nationalities were not to be crushed in defiance of international good faith by the arbitrary will of a strong and overmastering Power. He incidentally ,asked what we ourselves nihi exptet izoir the promise pf a •</p>
        <p>0, ',.,,', .1 V. • . k;''i. ~.'' . . ',lk.-'.,.; 4,;;:1..1.-:',... i..-Y4,, .:ik,k; i)., , 0 . .., ! . lt;r, ‘ ,:...i: , . 't,„; 44f, ~` 1.,:-,,,,... i'V, '.....- • 4.....,. ".,... + t.....,,,,. 1," • ~,...,. . ~,jl-i-7.4::,,,,,...v‘5- ',. - - ' -.-- • , . ,- 4 6-,t-vt.41.0, ,-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>ONE BRIGHT SPOT.</head>
        <p>THE ONE BRIGHT SPOT.</p>
        <p>There are many who think that the GERMAN EMPEROR, and those about him, believedthat England was so pre- occupied with her internal troubles that she would be unable to lend mate- rial aid to France. We scarcely think that can be so; but, in any case, they have seen how rapidly and solidly we can close our ranks in the face of an enemy. The one bright spot in the whole thunder-laden horizon, SIR EDWARD GREY said, was Ireland. Europe should understand that any division of views or clash of interests in that part of the British Empire has been forgotten in the Empire's need. Mr. REDMOND rose, after Mr. BONAR LAW, to drive this point home. The Government, he said, could withdraw every soldier from Ireland, and National would join hands with Ulster Volunteers in protecting the island from invasion. Mr. REDMOND'S declar- ation was received with the utmost enthusiasm, and there is no doubt that the-co-operation which he. suggested for helping Great Britain in her need, by relieving her of anxiety for the defence .of Ireland, would be gladly undertaken by the • two Volunteer organisations existing in Ireland to-day.</p>
        <p>Local War News. Scene: Near "The Examiner" Office; time, 11 p.m. Wednesday night. Group of four— two young Territorials and two very ordinary youths—earnestly discussing the respective merits of the belligerents, especially British and German. One Young Territorial (seriously): "But thgre are 80,000 Germans in Belgium." One Very Ordinary Youth: "What about that? Why, the Warrington 'coppers' cap- tured 17 Germans to-day !"-</p>
        <p>_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>PASSING WEEK.</p>
        <p>OUR DUTY.</p>
        <p>When Colonel FairelOugh, who is in command' of the Territorials, who have so handsomely responded to the call to arms, told the Deputy- Mayor, at the handing over of the colours on Thursday, that his Battalion were simply ' aoing their duty, and intended to do it, he did but reflect what should be, and doubtless is, the intention of us all. We•are not all going to the front, but we can all assist just as effec- tually, though perhaps not so self-saCrificingly, as the man who risks his life in his country's ' cause. The principal thing 'those who stay at home to do their duty have to remember is that money is the sinews of war. It has long been acknowledged that the nation that can: hold out longest financially must be the victor in modern warfare. We are the wealthiest nation on earth, and if we do not recklessly waste our resources we must wear down our adversaries, number their soldiers ever so many millions. The nian or Woman who in these circumstances ' wastes a sovereign is a traitor to the country and an effective, if unconscious, ally of the, enemy. Money can be wasted in many ways —by dribbling it away on drink and luxuries, by rushing to the banks for unneeded gold, by rushing to the shops for unneeded provisions, by the putting up of prices by tradesmen to benefit by the strange, unnatural rush for possession. To become panic-stricken over food and money is as senseless, and as fatal, as a panic in a theatre fire. With ordinary conduct the evil effects in either case may be got over. Just as a theatre empties itself slowly and safely in the absence of panic, so will the markets right themselves if we behave rationally. The Government have proviqed for the food supply through the safety of our shipping, the banks, with the Government be- hind them, have made the supply of money safe. To lose faith in the banks is to lose faith in ourselves.</p>
        <p>_ We are glad to say that although the shops were rushed in the eddy part of the week, now that the novelty of the occasion has somewhat worn off and it is known that there is plenty for all if the few do not get it, we are getting to a more normal state of things.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>PLENTY OF MONEY.</head>
        <p>PLENTY OF MONEY.</p>
        <p>When the banks opened yesterday, after a raeher long Bank Holiday, to allow of the new conditions being brought to perfection, there was little difference from the ordinary routine. That speaks volumes for the good sense of the people. That they may rest content, and are inclined to do so, is proved by the experience of the Savings Bank. Here the day passed with- out any extra demands being made by deposi- tors. As will be seen from a short article in another column, the National Debt Commis- sioners have written to say that if there should he any exceptional withdrawals at the Savings Bank the Commissioners will be prepared to meet the demand to any amount.</p>
        <p>The one pound notes have become popular at once, several of the customers of the Savings Bank have 'asked for them in preference •to gold.</p>
        <p>1` THE EXAMINER."</p>
        <p>We hope we may offer "The Examiner" as an example of what may be done. We usually appear as a twelve-page•paper ; to-day we are appearing as an eight-page gaper, thus, saving one-third in material. A few years ago an eight-page paper was accounted a good Pennyworth—the advance to twelve became a luxury in accord with the advances of the time. Now war has thrown us back a few years, and our readers, like ourselves, in cutting off-their luxuries will be but going back temporarily to a state of affairs which obtained not,very long ago. But we shall be "little and good," mak- ing the best of the circumstapces in which we are placed for the time being. -</p>
        <p>At the moment supplies of paper are plenti- ful and raw material in the country is adequate, but newspaper proprietors, while hoping, for the best, are preparing for the worst, a frame of mind in which the war should find all our countrymen and countrywomen. Save, con- serve, prevent waste, as though the fate of the nation and the Empire depended upon your every act, and, as we -said at the beginning, you will be doing as much to assist in crushing the German tyrant as if you had shouldered a gun against him. You will be providing the money to keep the gun going.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>TAKING OUR BEARINGS.</head>
        <p>TAKING OUR BEARINGS.</p>
        <p>It is satisfactory to note that the Mayor has called a meeting for *Monday evening to con- sider the local situation resulting from the war. The main need is that employers should, as far as possible, keep their people employed; even if short time should become necessary. It may be early to talk of distress works, but it would ;be Well for people to think of public work that could be carried out to the advantage of the cOmmUnity. Employers have treated their employes called to the war splendidly. Reduced wages to wives left behind, positions kept open for the time when workers may return, and similar kind- nesses have been shown—another example of effective assistance that can be rendered by the non-combatant.</p>
        <p>LOCAL PATRIOTISM. Our columns reveal the extent to which our local patriotistn has kept pace with the national patriotism. Reservists, Territorials, Ambulance men—all have answt+ed the call, and the fine, manly spirit that has been behind it all Inks made failure impossible.</p>
        <p>POLITICS BANNED. The political agents, Mr. Herbert Hayes and Mr. Ernest Sanders, have undertaken to aban- don all political propaganda work except regis- tration, which, of course, has to proceed, according to law.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>LIBER %LS AND THE WAR.</head>
        <p>LIBER %LS AND THE WAR.</p>
        <p>Mr. Crosfield writes to say triaT he agrees with every syllable of Sir Edward Grey's speech, and that Germany and Austria are alone responsible for this "hideous catastrophe." That, in summary, is the Liberal position, and we are glad to know that on this life and death question Conservatives are'at one with us. We have been forced into a horrible undertaking, and, being so forced, we will endeavour to make our adversary beware of us for the future.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>OTHER BRIGHT SPOTS.</head>
        <p>OTHER BRIGHT SPOTS.</p>
        <p>Another cause for congratulation is that we have not entered the fray in any mafficking spirit. We know the gravity. of the task we have taken on, and have entered upon it with a deter- mination to win, without unduly boast 7 ing. As we have indicated, all parties will strain their every nerve. The case of JOHN BURNS, who, as a peace advocate, has been forced to retire from the Government, is typical. He will still be useful at the Local Govern- ment Board in superintending mea- sures of relief, which will be 'as impor- tant and valuable as any work during the prosecution of the war. LORD KITCHENER has become a colleague Of ASQUITH and LLOYD GEORGE, and will preside t the War Office. We are solid, as, we believe. • the GERMAN EMOEROR will learn to his cost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>STAR KINEMA.</head>
        <p>STAR KINEMA.</p>
        <p>Amongst the pictures shown at the Star Kinema this week are the departure of the Women's Liberal Association's trip to Blackpool and Mr. Harold Smith's wedding. Next week " leost in Mid-ocean" will be the principal film for the first three days. and "In the Firelight" for the remainder of the week. i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>1914.</head>
        <p>1914.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON. WAR INCIDENTS.</p>
        <p>MOTOR CYCLES COMMANDEERED.</p>
        <p>The Army authorities have commandeered several motor cycles in Warrington and district.</p>
        <p>HEAVY RAIL TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>Although. -z3.7.g-realt U:Uffi.l-;er. of the -ordinary pas- senger trains have been cancelled, exceptionally heavy traffic has been passing through Warring- ton this week-end. PROMPTLY SUPPLIED. •</p>
        <p>Messrs. J. and W. Dutton performed a smart feat in the equipment of the local troops. They supplied 1,800 shirts and 1,600 pairs of socks within 24 hours. Mr. Roberts, boot and shoe dealer, as promptly equipped the local Terri- torials with footwear. A QUICK RETURN.</p>
        <p>A naval reservist who had this week just re- turned from training was re-called and within 15 hours of his arrival in Warrington had sailed upon his ship.</p>
        <p>The Bridge Footpaths. The Bridges Committee having suggested the advisability of increasing the width of the foot- paths over Warrington Bridge as planned, the Paving Committee have recommended that the original plans, allowing for 10ft, footpaths, be adhered to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>HOLIDAY-MAKERS' TROUBLES.</head>
        <p>HOLIDAY-MAKERS' TROUBLES.</p>
        <p>Warrington holiday-makers, who should have returned this week-end, have been rushing home earlier than expected, and found it extremely difficult to get back. One party from the Isle of Man took nearly twelve hours to get home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>PROCLAMATIONS TO CITIZENS.</head>
        <p>PROCLAMATIONS TO CITIZENS.</p>
        <p>The Mayor has received a number of procla- mations, which it is customary to issue in time of war. One removes the prohibition on the importation of arms into Ireland. BATHS FOR TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Baths will be closed to-day to the public, as they have been placed at the disposal of the Territorials. ALIENS IN WAFIRINGTON.</p>
        <p>We understand that quite a number of aliens have been registered at the Warrington Police Court, in accordance with the proclamation. MR. ROBERT PIERPOINT'S OFFER.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robt. Pierp-oint telegraphed yesterday tot the Mayor offering to place his house in Bold- street at the disposal of the town for the pur- pose of a hospital if required. A LANCASHIRE CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>- The Mayor will represent Warrington at a conference to be held at Preston on Monday. It has been convened by the Lord-Lieutenant of the County for the purpose of discBBft* Matters arising out of the war and making arrangemelts to deal with any distress that may arise.</p>
        <p>TROOPS DEPARTURE. The men who leave Warrington to-night are to assemble on the lawn in front of the Town nail at nine o'clock, and we are asked to request the public to assist the police in maintaining order. The Mayor will attend. The station will be barricaded during the departure of the-troops. RELIEF COMMITTEE FOR LYMM. At a meeting held last night of representa- tives of the VariollB places of worship to con- sider the formation of a local relief committee, Mr. Alfred Watkin presided. A committee was formed and it was stated that they were ready to deal with the question of relief immediately on receipt of instructions from the authorities. CLOTHING FOR BASE HOSPITAL. The Mayoress has asked the use of the Town Hall for the purpose of a company of ladies making clothing for use in the base hospital to be established in Liverpool. Mrs. Janet Bdel- sten, wife of Colonel Edelsten, states that a large number of ladies have offered to organise working parties. A meeting will be held shoitly in the Town Hall, which will deal with die matter.</p>
        <p>MORE RESERVISTS LEAVE. About 142 Resiirvists left Bank Quay Station last night for Tidworth. Reservists to the number of 864 have beeh in the Barracks, and there are now only 200 men left. Sixty addi- tiopal men have still to come up. The 3rd Battalion will leave for Great Crosby on Sunday morning. THE INFIRMARY TO TRAIN AMBULANCE</p>
        <p>VOLUNIIFERS. A joint meeting of the fikembers of the House Visitors and Finance Committee and the mem- bers of the Medical Board was held at the Infir- mary on Thursday, for the purpose of consider- ing how best the hospital authorities could assist in training the Daresbury Division Voluntary Aid Detachment of the British Red Cross Society, and the members of the War- rington Corps St. John Ambulance Brigade, who have volunteered to serve with the Royal Army Medical Corps. It was resolved : That permission be given to the Matron and Nursing Staff to assist in training the members of the Daresbur7 Division Voluntary, Aid De- tachments of the British Red Cross Society in the general work. of a hospital, by arranging for the members' attendance at convenient times in the accident and out-patients' depart- ments; That one of the private wards be placed at the society's disposal for practical training in work necessary for patients; and That if the various members prove themselves efficient, then it may be possible for them to receive further training in actual nursing in the wards. It was also resolved : _ - - -</p>
        <p>That-CO-n-sent-be.grven to the Hon. Surgeons and the House Surgeons in training the Volun- tary Aid Detachment of the St. John Ambulance Brigade, Warrington Corps, aa mach Pa Pos. sible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>Mayor as Forester.</head>
        <p>Mayor as Forester.</p>
        <p>- His Worship the Mayor has attended, as War- rington delegate, at the annual conference of the Ancient Order of Foresters held at Bourne- mouth this week. He returned to Warrington on Thursday.</p>
        <p>Catholic Boys' Brigade. The Warrington members of the Catholic Boys' Brigade returned from camp yesterday— a day earlier than anticipated, owing to the troublesome railway service. They; were in charge of Bandmaster Fox and the Chaplain (the Rev. Father B. Primavesi).</p>
        <p>Bridge Resident Engineer. The resignation of Mr. J M. Cobb, iesident engineer in charge of the Ow Warrington Bridge, upon his appointment to a Government position, has been accepted with regret; and Mr. A. G. Hodgson, who was formerly employed upon the bridge, has been appointed Mr. Cobb's successor upon the same terms.</p>
        <p>Mr. Charles Madeley. Mr. Charles Madeley was one of a deputation of the Museums' Association that waited upoa the Board of Education early this week on the subject of the increase of the circillati.an col- lections at the Victoria and Albert Museum. ,Mr. Pease, the President, was engaged with the Cabinet, presumably upon war business, hut the deputation was received by the perman- ent head of the Board, Sir H. Selby Bigge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>Howard Place.</head>
        <p>Howard Place.</p>
        <p>At the Town Council meeting on Tuesday the Sanitary Works Committee will report that they have accepted the tenders of Messrs. J. Broadhurst and Sons, for the erection of urinals in Lovely-lane at .£lO5, and in Howard- place at 1:300. _ _</p>
        <p>The Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee have been requested to undertake the planting of shrubs and trees in Howard-place, on the completion of the urinal.</p>
        <p>Brilliant Scholastic SuccesSes.. In the higher alternative papers of the joint alternative papers of the Joint Northern Uni- versity examination, H. C. Emmerson and William E. Mason both pass in Mathematics and Chemistry, and both obtain distinction in physics—being two out of five in the whole examination to do so. W. E. Mason has a pass in Latin (ordinary). These results are excep- tionally brilliant and reflect great credit upon the Secondary School staff.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>Extension of Victoria park.</head>
        <p>Extension of Victoria park.</p>
        <p>The Mayor will ;:e-poi:C to the 'Council next Tuesday that as a result of two interviews he has had with the agents of the late Earl of Ellesmere, he offered the sum of £4,150 for the purchase of 38 acres of land adjoining Victoria Park, and that that offer was accepted. The action of the Mayor has been approved by the General Purposes Committee, who re- commend an application' to the Local Govern- ment Board for their sanction to the borrowing of the purchase money.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Dwelling-house Fire.</head>
        <p>Dwelling-house Fire.</p>
        <p>Early on Thursday—afternoon the Warrington Fire Brigade was summoned to an outbreak at the dwelling-house, 44, Parker-street, occupied by Mrs. James Coyne. On the arrival of the motor fire engine, under the command of Super- intendent Turner, it was found that a lodger had succeeded in getting the fire extinguished. Considerable damage had been done, but the property damaged is not covered by insurance. The cause of the fire is unknown. Mrs. Coyne's husband has been called to the Colours, he being a Reservist.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Unprofitable Market Hall to be Demolished.</head>
        <p>Unprofitable Market Hall to be Demolished.</p>
        <p>The Markets Committee, having considered a resolution from the Street Improvement Com- mittee, requesting them to terminate the tenancies in the Market Hall so that the build- ings may be demolished, passed the following resolution: "That in the opinion of this com- mittee the Market Hall has become unprofitable and unsuitable and is no longer required for market purposes." To reinstate the tenants thus displaced it was resolved: "That the whole of the tenants in the Market Hall be allocated space on the south side of the fish stalls, that the Borough Sur- veyor prepare a plan showing the details, and that the Town Clerk give notice to the tenants of the committee's proposals."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>Late Mr. Bamforth.</head>
        <p>The Late Mr. Bamforth.</p>
        <p>We regret to announce the death of Mr. Joseph Bamforth, chemist, of 74, Winwick- street, which took place on Tuesday. Mr. Barn. forth, who was a prominent officer at St. Peter's Church, was very well known in the town and was respected by everyone with whom he came in contact. The funeral, which took place at the Warrington Cemetery yesterday, was pre- ceded by a service in St. Peter's Church, the Revs. B. Pervical and W. Bracecamp officiating. Tlie chief mourners were: Mr. Joseph Bamforth (son), Mr. Allan Baraforth (brother), Messrs. Arnold Bamforth and Sydney Yeomans knephews), Messrs. John and Ernest Sutcliffe (cousins), Messrs. Robert Young, J. H. Young (Birkenhead), and Alfred Worrall (Crewe).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>Draper's Debts.</head>
        <p>A Draper's Debts.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Bankruptcy , Court, yes- terday, Herman- Shapira, draper, Winwick- street, appeared for his public examination. The liabilities were £876 is., and assets ,2122 4s. 2d., ' leaving a deficiency of -e754 2s. 10d. Debtor attributed his failure to lack of capital, bad trade, heavy travelling expenses, and illness of wife 4nd children.—ln answer to Mr. Murga- troyd, the assistant official receiver, debtor said that he came from Roumania 17 years ago. He I had attended various markets, including War- rington, and he had also conducted businesses in Winwick-street, Green-street and Bold-street, Warrington. He had had to give up the Bold- street and Green-street shops because they did not pay. He admitted that he had put the following sums in the hank: 1911, ..C1,700; 1912, ,;C1,918; 1913, ,L 2,064 but said his expenses were too heavy for his businesses to be successful. He had borrowed money from a brother-in-law. He was on a lease and could not give the busi- ness up before he did.—The examination was closed.</p>
        <p>Importance of Museum Work. Alderman Bennett, deputy chairman of the Museums Committee, who recently attended the conference of the Aluseums' Association at Swansea, reports that the occasion was rendered additionally interesting by the fact that " the President for the year was our esteemed cura- tor, Mr. Charles Madeley. The associate mem- bers attending included Mr. Thomas May, a member of our committee, whose work for War- rington will always be gratefully remembered, and Mr. Stuart Page, of Warrington; whilst I am glad to record the presence of Mr. E. E. Lowe, B.Sc., the curator of the Leicester Museum and honorary secretary of the associa- tion, and Mr. Arthur Deane, the curator of the Belfast Museum, both of whom received their early training under Mr. Madeley." After reviewing the work done, Alderman Bennett concludes: "This is the first annual conference of the Museums' Association which it has been my good fortune to attend; and I come back to my native town with a redoubled sense of the importance of our work, and a re- newed resolve that the Warrington Museum, which is, as Mr. Madeley minted out, the first rate-sup-ported museum in the United Kingdom, shall yet be housed in an edifice adequate for all the developments which will be needed in order to make it fully harmonise with his com- prehensive and inspiring definition."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>Dodging the Crowd.</head>
        <p>Dodging the Crowd.</p>
        <p>"Have you ever loved before?" asked the coy maid. ,</p>
        <p>"Yes," yawned the worldly young man, "but --er—never before a chaperon, two small brothers and a pet bulldog." And then she suggested a trip down the old road to see the stars.</p>
        <p>.-••••'""</p>
