      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>SATTJRD</head>
        <p>SATTJRD</p>
        <p>~-7.,.....A.v.0.V.5T.....' 8,</p>
        <p>Football in Victoria Park.</p>
        <p>Instructions have been given for the laying out of an additional Rugby football ground, and the erection of a small shed for the use of the players at Victoria Park.</p>
        <p>Ready for Fire. The Inspector, of Hydrants has made an in- spection of the fire appliances at the Infirmary, Town Hall, Parr Hall, Museum and Library, Technical School, School of Art, and Fever Hospital; and has found them "clean and in good working order."</p>
        <p>The Priory School: Music Success. At the recent examination of the Associated Board of the Royal Academy of Music and the Royal College of Music, held at Chester, Phyllis Ellis, pupil at the above school, successfully passed in the Elementary Division.</p>
        <p>Lighting Warrington Bridge. The Street Improvement Committee have approved the plan and specification for lighting Warrington Bridge with electricity; and the Bridges Committee have resolved to raise the cost—.QBs--upon loan.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>Increased Dangers of the Road.</head>
        <p>Increased Dangers of the Road.</p>
        <p>At a recent meeting—of the Watch Committee a letter from the Warrington Trades and Labour Council, with reference to the increased danger to drivers of horse-drawn vehicles, caused through increased motor traffic, was read and referred to the Town Clerk.</p>
        <p>Increased Baths Receipts. For the fortnight ended July 9th the income from the baths was £72 Os. 2d., against £36 13s. in the corresponding •period last year; and for the fortnight ended July 23rd they were £7l 195., against £25 6s. Increase for the month, £B2 Os. 2d.</p>
        <p>Compensation. The Street Improvement Committee have re- solved that the sum of £B3O be paid to Messrs. Greensll, Whitley and Co., Ltd., being the sum awarded by the late Mr William Owen as,com- pensation to be paid to the company for setting back to the prescribed line the premises for- merly known as the Bowling Green Inn, Liver- pool-road.</p>
        <p>2,17,000 for Street Widening. The Finance and Estate Committee have re- ceived the- sanction of the Local Government Board to the borrowing of the sum of ,R 17,26.3 for the purchase of land for the widening of Bridge-street, Buttermarket-street, Sankey. street and Horsemarket-street. The loan has to .be repaid within a period not exceeding sixty years.</p>
        <p>Borough Prize Brass Band. The above band will play the following pro- gramme of music in the Park to-morrow : Hymn, "Happy Hearts," J. A. Greenwood; march, "Hand and Hearts," J. A. Greenwood; selection, "Le pre aux Cleres," Herold; selec- tion, "Martyrs," Donizetti; fantasia, "Son of the Lea," W. Rimmer; chorus, "Comrades in Arms," A. Adams; "God Save the King." Conductor, Mr. T. Griffin.</p>
        <p>bank holiday the County Court fixed for Thursday morning— when Judge 11. E. Gurner would have sat in Warrington for the first time—was postponed until August 17th. The following notice was posted outside the buildings: "This office will be closed on Wednesday, sth August, and Thurs- day, 6th August, 1914, by order of the Lord Chancellor.—T. J. Ridgway, registrar." the ele extension C.Olll</p>
        <p>Co-operative Stores' Procession. The Chief Constable having reported to the Watch Committee that the Warrington E.I. Co- ' operative Society, Ltd., had intimated their in- tention of having a procession of children from Bank Park to the Football Field on Saturday, August Bth, in connection with their children's sports, it was resolved "that the necessary ar- rangements be left in the hands of the Chief Constable."</p>
        <p>Presentation to Mr. Arthur Siddall. To mark his approaching marriage to Miss Laura Hill, of Cressington, Mr. A thur Siddall, foreman at the King-street wc: of Mr. G. Davey-Smith, and a.well-known in,aiber of the Warrington A.C., was recently made the recip- ient of a fumed oak bureau and circular table with fall leaves, subscribed for by his fellow- workers. Mr. Davey-Smith paid a high tribute to Mr. Siddall, and offered congratulations, and later Miss E. M. Bennett voiced the good wishes of the staff. The wedding takes place to-morrow at Grassendale Church.</p>
        <p>Corporation Masons' Wages. It has been decided to pay the masons in the employ of .the Corporation at the rate of 9d. per hour and in itccordance with union condi- tions.</p>
        <p>A petition from the paviors in the employ of the Corporation with regard to the rate of wages was read at a recent meeting of the Pav- ing Committee, and no action was taken thereon.</p>
        <p>Working Men at the Infirmary. The members of the Working Men's Infirmary Committee, who work so hard throughout the year to raise funds for the upkeep of our princi- pal charitable institution, were invited, along with their wives or lady friends, to inspect the building and wards last Saturday afternoon. The party, a large one, was welcomed in the hall by the President, Sir Gilbert Greenall, who was accompanied by Mr. J. H. Smethurst (chair- man of the Board of Management), Mr. George Walker (vice-chairman Working Men's Com- mittee), Messrs. W. Daintith, A. C. Binks (representatives on the Board of Management of the Working Men's Committee), and J. H. J. Hampson (hon. sec. of the committee). Alder- man T. Burton (chairman of the Working Men's Committee) was unable to be present in conse- quence of indisposition. A tour of the whole institution was made and all that was seen gave the utmost satisfaction to the visitors. On the lawn, subsequently, Mr. Walker and Mr. Binks thanked Sir Gilbert for his kindness in attending to receive them, and referred in appreciative terms to the interest he and Lady Greenall took in such work as that done by the Infirmary. Sir Gilbert expressed his pleasure at being able to do all he could to encourage the com- mittee in the work they had in hand; and he wag sure they had seen enough that day to con- vince them that their efforts were not in vain. The working men of Warrington had done their duty nobly, and lie had sin doubt they would con- tinue to do so. lie thanked them for their kindly reference to himself and Lady Greenall.</p>
        <p>Children's Summer Catnp. The seriousness of the European War does not , seem to overwhelm the one hundred and thirty- six Warrington boys who are at present in camp in the quiet little spot near Prestatyn, although discussions have arisen as to the pro- bability of "staying here a month"." Prospects of this kind seem to greatly please the lads, and although of rather a romantic character, indi- cate the content and happiness which prevails. Saturday and Sunday were wet days, and rather damped the ardour of the boys, but since then the weather has been ideal. A.n attempt was made on Saturday, during the heavy rain, to hold a concert, which turned ouit a great success. The programme was supplied by both officers and lads, and comprised solos (comic and otherwise), choruses, and recitations. The "star • thin" was a song entitled "The Empress of Ireland," sung by T. Gagen. During the week opportunities have been given to the lads to indulge in sea bathing, and the usual walks have been taken: (1) over the hills, and (2) to Dyserth. Thursday is au' aye a red-letter day for the lads, who keenly take part in sports organised by the officers. On this occasion the various races acre sharply contested and greatly enjoyed. It must 1..?t3 men- tioned that the prizes in many eases -4 ere sup- I plied by generous-hearted tradespeople of Prestatyn, to whom all tender their sincerest ' thanks. It is to be hoped that the week's holiday by the sea will invigorate the lads, and that they will return benefited by their holiday.</p>
        <p>The officers in charge are as follows: Messrs. W. H. Houghton (superintendent), Appleton, Farrar, Turner, Barratt, Emmerson and . .</p>
        <p>Xnapecior Wain Wright.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
