      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <p>recognise her neutrality according to treaty obligations while at war with France.</p>
        <p>" Infamous proposal," was the epithet applied by Mr. Asquith to the German scheme. It was an invitation, lie declared, to betray our friends and dishonour our obligations. What were we to get in return for the proposed bargain? the Premier asked. "A promise given by a Power which was at that very moment announcing its intention to violate its own treaty obligations." Mr. Asquith declared that a nation never entered into a great contest With a clearer con- science. His declaration received the warm ap- proval of Mr. Bonar Law in the Commons and Lord Lansdowne in the Second Chamber.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>INCREASING_ THE. ARMY AND</head>
        <p>INCREASING_ THE. ARMY AND</p>
        <p>NAVY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Asquith also asked on behalf of Lord Kitchener, the new War Secretary, for Power to increase the Army by half a million men, and for 67,000 more men for the Navy.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>HEALTH INSURANCE OF RESERVES AND TERRITORIALS.</head>
        <p>HEALTH INSURANCE OF RESERVES AND TERRITORIALS.</p>
        <p>Mode of Procedure.</p>
        <p>Contributions during embodied service will be at reduced Army rate of 3d. a week, lid. of which will be deducted from pay. They will be paid on special Army (B) cards, supplied by Military authorities. As regards 'ordinary cards for current half- year, Reservists should fill in the name of the society and branch (if any) and deposit cards at Military Depot in the box provided for the pur- pose; Territorials should send their cards to their societies, writing across them the word "Territorial.'l Cards left in custody of em- ployers should be kept until further notice unless_ claimed by the man.</p>
        <p>During embodied service any claim to Mater- nity Benefit should be made by the wife to the Approved Society; or, in the case of Deposit Contributors, to the Insurance Committee, in the ordinary way.</p>
        <p>‘A'ARRINGTON POLICE AND THE WAR.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Illen IVho have Joined the Colours.</head>
        <p>Illen IVho have Joined the Colours.</p>
        <p>Owing to the mobilisation of the Army Re- serves the 'Warrington Police Force has been depleted, eight of the constables being called up. The names of the men, together with their regiments and the place where they join them, are as follows: P.C. Bromwich, 2nd Life Guards, Windsor.</p>
        <p>P.C. Grubb, Berkshire Regiment, Reading. P.C. Cook, 2nd Life Guards, Windsor. J.C. Cutler, R.F.A., Glasgow. P.C. Prentice, Royal Horse Guards, London. P.C. Skinner, Coldstream Guards, London. P.C. Borrows, Grenadier Guards, London. P.C. Adcock, Coldstream Guards, London. The first two men are married.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>POLICE RECRUITS.</head>
        <p>POLICE RECRUITS.</p>
        <p>We understand that the Chief Constable, Mr. Nicholls, is preparing a list of men who would be willing to act as special constables in War- rington, if, and when, required, with payment for services, and will interview applicants at the Police Station. at any time. .</p>
        <p>_ Any young men desirous of joining as ordinary constables will also be seen by Mr. Nicholls any day, including all day Sunday, and the Chief Constable will be glad to give them every assist- ance possible, as he knows of several forces where men are at present wanted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>AUSTRIA DECLARES WAR.</head>
        <p>AUSTRIA DECLARES WAR.</p>
        <p>Austria has declared war on Russia. On the eastern 'frontiers of Germany and Austria Russian patrols are in contact with the enemy. No important events in the eastern theatre are to be looked for during the next two weeks or more. The Russian mobilisation is said to be proceeding more rapidly than was expected.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <p>full strength would provide the larger force. In peace time two of the corps engaged are stationed at Munster and Hanover. They have been moved south-south-west to their present line of advance. This indicates a southward concentration of the German Aimies, perhaps to march upon the French through Luxemburg and Lorraine. The third corps mentioned in the telegrams is stationed in peace time at Breslau, in South-Eastern Prussia, near the Russian Vorder.</p>
        <p>Very heavy fighting has taken place around the fortifications, in which the Belgians held their own. Belgian reports say the German losses are serious. Seven guns fell into the defenders' hands and many prisoners, who were sent to Brussels. Some German horsemen, greatly daring, penetrated far into the city, where they were all killed. At mid-day on ,Thursday Liege was still completely in Belgian control. Since speed is the essence of Ger- many's plan, for she must make haste through Belgium if she is to catch grance unprepared, the check before a frontier fortress means much. According to some accounts five German Army Corps are now involved in the corner of a country which was expected to be neutral. An announcement issued from the French Ministry of War says the fighting before Liege was continuing fiercely on Thursday afternoon. Two forts had been silenced, but the defence was continued. Already the Belgian Army</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>TO MEET POSSIBLE DISTRESS. Meeting Called by the Mayor.</head>
        <p>TO MEET POSSIBLE DISTRESS. Meeting Called by the Mayor.</p>
        <p>• The following circular letter has been issued to employers of labour and others interested : Town Hall, Warrington, August 5, 1914. Dear Sir,—ln view of the Declaration of War with Germany and the serious consequences which may follow by the calling up of the Re- servists and Territorials, and the probable lack of employment owing to dislocation of trade, I should be glad if you would meet me at the Town Hall on Monday evening next at 7.30 for the purpose of considering the steps to be taken to alleviate distress and to provide employment, and to appoint a committee to give effect to the decision of the meeting. Yours faithfully, PETERI PEACOCK, Mayor.</p>
        <p>To the Editor.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln view of the present crisis everyone will want to serve his country in some way. Distress is likely to be keen in the near future, and I may say at once that the Guild of Help will be glad to take its part in any concerted measures that may be taken for the relief of distress. I will not attempt to forestall any suggestions that may be made at the Town Hall meeting on Monday concerning public action. I should like to suggest, however, that each comfortable household can contribute to the general good by adopting a simple scale of living</p>
        <p>Photograph by Burrell.</p>
        <p>ONE OF THE FIRST CAPTURES OF THE WAR. German Vessel at Walton Lay-by. The German timber boat Dyrade, lying at Walton lay-by, was seized by the Custom officials on Wednesday. Seventeen officers and men have been released on parole, and the police are in charge of the ship. It was reported last night that another ship, which had German and Austrian sailors on board, had been help up at Acton Grange.</p>
        <p>* iWI ( , , , • , ' 11111 . , • , s• Tb .. . 4 . , , , • 44A Willikilea-_,Adh. s' .)..'k/'—'----- '—.. , ''' ' ' -', '',.' ' • I , , &gt;•,',' L • ', ' - '' ' --, - , ''',:, '' • ' ' ' ° , ' `Z4s, , .: ~,,4 ,''.!f ,‘,'", ''',,t, -.:,,'t''' ',''',i, ,' 0" ~,, ',s • "„ ' ',. • ..' * , ~ ,'• -• ',, , ',, '.l':*'4'.i.' ‘'' i. ~ ,',, ~ ~', ~ `'.4.,*•.:' ~ , , ~, '-',, , . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <p>had delayed the German advance for 36 hours, and by so doing had gravely hampered the German leaders in the carrying out of their plans. Last night it was reported that the Germans had lost 25,000 men, and had asked for an armistice of 24, hours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>BRITISH CRUISER SUNK.</head>
        <p>BRITISH CRUISER SUNK.</p>
        <p>The British cruiser Amphion has gone down after striking a mine. She was the ship to which was at first attributed the sinking of the Koenigin Luise.</p>
        <p>The German mine-layer Koenigin Luise was sunk by the 'destroyer Lance, which caught the enemy in the act of laying mines sixty miles from Harwich and near the Dutch coast, and fired many shells. Twenty-eight Germans, many seriously wounded, were rescued, and are now prisoners at Harwiph. The Secretary of the Admiralty on Thursday</p>
        <p>and definitely laying aside a certain sum of money for the benefit of poorer neighbours each week while the trouble lasts. No doubt the Cost of provisions will be increased, but if economies are effected in the scale of living, something may be put on one side to share with others.</p>
        <p>Some households will not be able to do much, but a saving of even threepence a day will suffice to give a quart of milk daily to some poor family. Just one word about the food supply. It was perfectly natural that people should rush to the provision shops in the first exciting moments. But now there ought to be no further buying in excess of present needs. If we pray for daily bread and then buy enough flour to last a month, we are preventing poorer people from getting their share. The huge demand for fond has done as much to enhance food prices as the passing shortness of supplies. After all there is no need for panic. The Atlantic is open and sellers are quite as eager to sell to Britain as she is to buy. WILFRID J. ROWLAND.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <p>._ . . . . . . _ . . . . .."•7'. • ' THE EILAMINhR, SATURDAY, AUGUST ' 'B, .1914. • . . •• ... . . . I. . , 4 THE GREATEST WAR IN \ . , ~ ,</p>
