      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>TERRITORIAL COLOURS</head>
        <p>TERRITORIAL COLOURS</p>
        <p>Handed Over for Safe Custody to the Town. GREAT DEEDS PAST AND FUTURE.</p>
        <p>An historic scene was witnessed on Thursday morning in front of the Town Hall steps, when the colours of the local Territorials (the 4th Battalion South Lancashire Ilegiment) were delivered to the Deputy-Mayor (Dr. Joseph), in the absence of the Mayor (who was in Bourne- . . _</p>
        <p>mouth on Friendly Society businoss), for safe custody during the absence of the battalion from the borough. Among those present, in addition to the Deputy-Mayor, were: Aldermen W. Pier- point, J. Smethurst, D. Tinnion, James Evans, John Evans, T. Burton; Councillors E. Osborne, J. G. Craik, J. Skelland, J. Dickenson, J. Halli- well, S. Gough, J. Chrimes; Messrs. J. Lyon Whittle (town clerk), A. T. Hallaway (deputy town clerk), John Fairhurst (borough treas- urer), W. 11. Woodcock (assistant overseer), A. M. Ker (borough surveyor), Matthew A. Piercy (deputy borough surveyor), F. E. White (magis- trates' clerk), Dr. G. W. N. Joseph (medical officer of health), F. G. Ruddock (public analyst), Chas. Madeley (librarian), G. A. Dun- lop (sub-curator), James Gray (water engineer), J. Telfer (tram manager), J. H. J. Hampson. (secretary Infirmary), Roger Pierpoint, R. G. Pierpoint, and others, including a number of ladies. The King's colour was borne by Lieut. W. McClure, and the regimental colour by Lieut. Eric Fairclough, with an escorting party, con- sisting of three sergeants, Sergt.-Major Ather- ton, and Sergt.-Drummer Clarke. Colonel Fairclough and Captains Appleton owl Crosfield accompanied the party. The party having marched from the Drill Hall to the Town Hall, they were received by the Deputy-Mayor. Colonel Fairclough, addressing Dr. Joseph, said his battalion would consider it a great favour if the Mayor would take the colours in safe keeping during their absence. The colour party, at the request of the Colonel, advanced with the colours, and Dr. Joseph, hay- ing formally accepted them, ordered their re- moval to a room inside the Hall.</p>
        <p>CONFIDENCE OF THE TOWN. The Deputy-Mayor, addressing the Colonel and the Small detachment, said that it devolved upon him as representing the Mayor and Cor- poration of Warrington, to receive the colours— emblems of great deeds in the past, and, he hoped, of similar deeds in the future. In this the darkest hour, or, at any rate, the most critical time in the history of our country, it would ill-become us to treat this ceremony in anything but a spirit of solemnit47—but there were encouraging points even here. First, they were a real factor in the active British Army. (Hear; hear.) They were about to engage 'and give their services in a noble and holy cause. More, he would say that they had the entire confidence and trust of their fellow- townsmen—(applause)—that they would do the utmost in their power to maintain' the high traditions and great achievements of their pre- decessors.</p>
        <p>"I now wish you God speed,'" concluded Dr. Joseph, "and God bless you'; and, I would say 'God Save the King.'"</p>
        <p>(Cheers.) SIMPLY CALLED TO DUTY. Colonel Fairclough thanked the Deputy-Mayor for the exceedingly kind way in which he had accepted the colours. It would indeed help then a very greatly to feel that what they were able to do in behalf of their country would be appreciated in the spirit in which the Deputy Mayor had indicated. He and his battalion were simply called to duty. They sincerely hoped and trusted that they would not fail to do that duty. (Cheers.)</p>
        <p>At the call of Alderman Smethurst, cheers were given for the battalion, and the party returned to the Drill Hall.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Nat the Co-operatin Noised is doing for its Members in Warrington.</head>
        <p>Nat the Co-operatin Noised is doing for its Members in Warrington.</p>
        <p>In a time of great crisis, whilst retail prices of all food stuffs have been going up by leaps and bounds, and the ordinary trader (who has in the bulk of instances not paid any advanced prices for the goods he has been selling) has been reaping fabulous profits at the expense of his customers,</p>
        <p>THE CO-OPERATIVE SOCIETY HAS NOT ADVANCED PRICES OF FOOD STUFFS.AT ALL. Our prices for Butter have been 1/3 and 1/4 per lb., whilst others have advanced to 1/6 and even 1/8. Sugar has been sold at 2d., 2,1-d. and 21d., according to whether it is Crystal, Granu- lated, or Cube, whilst 4d., 5d., and 6d. per lb. is asked and obtained in other shops. Bacon is still retailed as before.</p>
        <p>_ The only thing withdrawn from sale has been Flour, and this has been done to enable us to keep the Bakery running, and</p>
        <p>TO KEEP BREAD DOWN TO PRESENT PRICES.</p>
        <p>We do not know how long we can maintain these prices, because Contracts have been broken and supplies are difficult to obtain, but our Members can rest assured that no attempt will be made by the local Society to obtain additional profit at the expense of its members.</p>
        <p>We wish to point out that the Co-operative Society belongs to its Members, and is being run in their interests as a whole, and their whole-hearted support is asked for in these diffi- cult times.</p>
        <p>It has been found necessary to LIMIT THE GOODS SUPPLIED TO MEMBERS,</p>
        <p>because unfortunately panic buying set in, and endeavours were being made in all directions to purchase food stuffs far beyond present require- ments.</p>
        <p>SO LONG AS WE CAN OBTAIN GOODS, NO ONE OF OUR MEMBERS WILL GO lINSUPPLIED, but we cannot lend ourselves to the few hoard- ing up at the expense of the many. REGARDING MONEY. Many members have rushed to get their Share Capital. We regret their action, BUT THEY HAVE BEEN PAID. The Committee have decided to pay any sums up to £5 on demand, and other larger with- drawals at their discretion. We ask your suP- port. If you do not actually require your Cash, don't draw it. We are seeking to show the working classes where their interest lies. Their combined Capital has enabled us to obtain lairaasa enabled b theyled I ncial posi all supplies of goods for their use. Their trade and our general fina Twhi el a st a vcion: go ps distress. members will help them ind in toon n What n erring on zet obektenegp inflated nfoarrmparli,v private egna gain. 11 the future still more. and IS doing, it can do in as in past years y JAMES .TARMAN, Secretary. emraatdioenof b</p>
        <p>Pi</p>
        <p>a</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Ym.....1•••=,..M.M.•••MM....14•MM,••</head>
        <p>Ym.....1•••=,..M.M.•••MM....14•MM,••</p>
        <p>The Polite Sergeant., "So you lost your watch? Did you report it to the detectives?" asks the friend. "Yes. Just came from headquarters," an- swers the man. "Got any suspicion as to who took it?" "I didn't have until after the sergeant got through questioning me about it. Now I SUS- pect myself of stealing it."</p>
      </div>
