      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <p>"This send-off is splendid, fine—it could not be beaten anywhere."</p>
        <p>These words were expressed by Captain Herbert to "The Examiner" representative, when, accompanied by the hearty cheers and good wishes of the people of Warrington, 400 Reservists of the South Lancashire Regiment marched from Orford Barracks on Thursday night to Bank Quay Station, where they entrained for Tidworth, a military station on Salisbury Plain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>AT THE BARRACKS.</head>
        <p>AT THE BARRACKS.</p>
        <p>For hours before the departure a large crowd had been assembling outside Orford Barracks, and they waited with patience to give the first civilian cheer to the soldiers as they emerged from their headquarters. There was quite a contrast inside the Barracks, for there there was no cheering, the men in khaki patrolling the square in small parties, some talking about the war, and even some about the latest cricket news!</p>
        <p>Occasionally the huge swinging gates were opened to admit a motor car or a cart, and then the throng outside attempted to get a glimpse of the soldiers, but nothing could be seen, for barrack squares are not the best lighted of places. The high men's quarters were sil- houetted against a sky that possessed but little illumination, and a relief was formed by the lights at all the windows. There was no voice raised above the ordinary conversational tone, and even this hushed for a moment when the massed buglers sounged the " naming for parade."</p>
        <p>, A few minutes later the sound of drums, and ' bugles arrested attention, and ae seventy Boy Scouts, in charge of Assistant Scoutthasters Young and Garner neared the entrance, the men .of the military flecked to - meet them. These Scouts—"Crosfield's Own"—were admired by all. Their errand was to carry torches at the head of the procession when it began to move. Soon after the torches- had been set alight it was observed that the soldiers were already carrying their kit, and following the receipt of their rifles- they were marshalled 9n to the lawn facing the officers' quarters. Patriotic words were given to the men, who received them with wild cheers—the first that night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>MAYOR PRESENT.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR PRESENT.</p>
        <p>A numbei.-(YTe.;;i-tOri-al officers had made an appearance, and the Mayor (Councillor Peter Peacock) was present. His Worship had only arrived in town at eight o'clock that evening, and had previously paid a visit to the local Territorials. Owing to the depleted train ser- vice he had been over twelve hours on his journey from Bournemouth, where he was at- tending a conference of the Ancient Order of Foresters—all unaware of the doings in War- rington, despite his repeated attempts to get into communication. He was not in touch with any arrangements in the town, and, feeling anxious, he obtained the special permission to leave the conference.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>SOLDIERS ON THE MARCH.</head>
        <p>THE SOLDIERS ON THE MARCH.</p>
        <p>Crosfield's Band was at the head of the line, and as the detachment moved off struck up the " 3larseillaise," but little of the tune was audible—it was almost drowned by the cheering. Inside the gateway, soldiers who are to go later cheered those who were going then, and hearty good wishes were expressed by all. The lights, carried aloft by the Scouts, looked majestic, I and heralded to the thousands of people in the streets the approach of the men, who are anxious and willing to fight England's battles. The tremendous cheer with which the troops left the Barracks never slackened all along the route; here and there, where people were thin- nest, there was a lull, but mighty shouts marked the spots where thousands were assembled. The Mayor marched along with the soldiers. On his one hand was Captain Herbert—who was in charge—and on his other Captain Cotton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>"RULE, BRITANNIA."</head>
        <p>"RULE, BRITANNIA."</p>
        <p>Down Orford-lane and Winwick-street there were tremendous crowds, and at the Central Station the torches revealed faces peering over the bridge and from the high walls. It was at ;this spot, with the crowd all moving with the ,troops, that the band broke forth with "Rule, ;Britannia," a song which was taken up by 'everybody. At Market Gate, in Sankey-street, ;and in Parker-street, loyal Warringtonians 'cheered to the echo, and as the soldiers passed on to the station the National Anthem ,was played. _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>AT THE STATION.</head>
        <p>AT THE STATION.</p>
        <p>- A large posse of police was on duty at the station approach to prevent the crowd reaching the platform. In the booking hall was Lieu- tenant Salter, who had come, from Tidworth to meet the four companies, and his general hearty handshakes and merry words seemed much appreciated. A touching scene was witnessed , at the barrier, for here a soldier •bade " good- , bye" to the young woman he had the previous day made his wife, and although they were touched with grief they bore up bravely, parting with a kiss and hoping for the best. All the other men seemed happy enough, and it ap- peared by their faces that they were going to a music-hall instead of to the Theatre of War. A fekr minutes before midnight the half- battalion steamed out of the station, returning the cheers of the few people on the platform, including Major Green and the Mayor.</p>
        <p>PRAISE FOR THE MEN. A detachment left the Barracks the previous night, under the command of Captain Evans. Major Green, who is in command of the Barracks, the depot of the "South Lancashires" in an interview, said the manner in which the men had come in had been surprising— "most extraordinary," to use his own words. Between the order to mobilise—on Wednesday night—and Thursday night, 829 men had turned in, which left only 120 to be dealt with later. He stated that the greatest credit was due to the men, who were very enthusiastic.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>TERRITORIAL COLOURS.</head>
        <p>TERRITORIAL COLOURS.</p>
        <p>• dAk ,gftt. . . !-.."..-/' ''''• .i&gt;'-it.•,.f,i1,..:'''.;,!:',•.i.,g,,,,1%--...' ..' t.,•.,, ' ;....,.:.,'‘.:,„' ;.• ' . ~.'-••'....11,t 'Ai-;;'..,,,,,,,'/14. '.'.. ''''' ::. , :`..''':'...':::.„!''''t•,,,t 10, r. -I.:_L t'.: : . 1 • .::"'-''..„-. f ~,i.,/:',.,1-,--,•'-','- ....„-, •.....,,..i',,:,'",',...,..',„:,-.:-?:...:: . ' ',,, 0 : :,....,;..,..t.;,.1.,'.....' •;,:'''''',:;',-. -.-...: , - ..,. 4.0 tj, ~.. ... ,t t ~.,;.t. /. - ft -F. 7 ..4,..,......,-.4t1.4 ~..',.-: ...,:..-4 '. t.4!‘„.5',it,r,,i,'" ..-- •.:ititi:.4, 1'..-, ._ ;4' ' ~ ~... i'. .-0 . ' W . :,', .;?'.";6:'i'''',.c; . •",.... ' y.i. '- ' t - - • ' 1504 .. 11,11'.. ~ x",,'t., t-.,'..,t:t.•,:,:it:-.1 ,-,,.,.'.' ...:., ..,,,,,:. t,, t ....- .t.....---- .'..,:-.;,..',.:tt.,,,;'-..,....'•:,•.'t•-;•,,,it..-- .....A, c'..... . ' - , 0,5,,,,,..,', ~.,„...', ....,..... - ..,-, • , ~. ..., . . ~.....,. . ••„ ... .... ..,, .„... . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Photograph by Burrell. HANDED OVER FOR SAFE KEEPING AT THE TOWN HALL.</head>
        <p>Photograph by Burrell. HANDED OVER FOR SAFE KEEPING AT THE TOWN HALL.</p>
        <p>Ambulance Brigade was held at the Police Station on Wednesday night to consider the situation. Among those present were Drs. Law and Manson, and Messrs. Baldry, Williams and Spann, officers in the Brigade and the Voluntary -Aid Detachment.</p>
        <p>It was explained that in the serious work that now lay before this country a great part had to be played far away from the battlefield—the task of bringing succour to the wounded and making them available for further active ser- vice.</p>
        <p>The division had been asked to say how many volunteers among ambulance men could be got on the following conditions:—</p>
        <p>" Individuals-might be required to sign an agreement, which could be renewed from year to year, to the effect that in the event of the mobilisation of the Territorial Forces they are willing immediately to enlist in the Royal Army Medical Corps of the Territorial Forces for one year or for the duration of the war. A copy of the agreement will be handed to the individual."</p>
        <p>The thirty-three officers and men present unanimously decided to volunteer, and Dr. Law promised to endeavour to have arrangements made by which they could have some practical experience at the Infirmary before their de- parture for active service. RADDON COURT AS MILITARY HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>....--. • The Daresbury Division of the Red Cross Society have had placed at their disposal by Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Co., the large house, Paddon Court, Knutsford-road, which, under the supervision of Mrs. F. A. Frost, the quartermaster, is being equipped as a military hospital to receive forty patients. Liberal assistance has been extended from many quar- ters. Lady Greenall is equipping ten beds, and the ladies of the division are making shirts and night shirts, which have been cut out, free, by Messrs. T. and W. Dutton. Furniture has also been promised, and everyone, says Mrs. Frost, has been very kind. Five nurses will be in charge of the hospital. More and More.</p>
        <p>Travelling Inspector of Sclmols (after\ severely cross-questioning the terrified class): And now, boys, who wrote Hamlet?</p>
        <p>Timid Boy: P-p-please, sir, it wasn't me. Travelling Inspector (the same evening to his host, ,the squire of the village) Most amusing thing happened to-day. I was questioning the class and asked a boy who wrote Hamlet, and he answered tearfully, "P -p-please, sir, it wasn't me."</p>
        <p>Squire (after loud and prolonged laughter): Ha, ha! That's good; and I suppose the little devil had done it all the time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>AMBULANCE MEN. Respond Readily and Unanimously to the Call. CROSFIELD'S RESERVISTS DEPART.</head>
        <p>AMBULANCE MEN. Respond Readily and Unanimously to the Call. CROSFIELD'S RESERVISTS DEPART.</p>
        <p>The Crosfield Division of the St. John Ambu- lance Brigade was called upon on Saturday night by headquarters to be in readiness to send their Royal Naval Sick Berth Reserves to Devonport, and the reply was that all the reservists were ready at call. Shortly after one o'clock on Monday morning that call came, and Superintendent J. Rose and First Officer Cawley at once made a tour of the men's houses, and the contingent, 29 in all, left by the 1.46 train on Monday afternoon, just 12 hours after the receipt of orders. FIRM PROUD OF THE MEN.</p>
        <p>Captain Crosfield inspected the men in the works yard before their departure for the sta- tion and told them that the firm, who were proud of the fact that 29 of their employes had answered their country's call so promptly,, would keep their situations open for them. The Reserves are divided into four classes: (a) for service on a battleship; (b) for service on hospital ships, conveying wounded from battleships to hospitals; (o) for service in for- eign hospitals; (d) for service in home hospitals. The Crosfield contingent is made up as follows: Class A.—Sergeant H. Flood, Corporals Mellor and Webb, Privates Fisher, Peters, Ganon, Holding and Hughes.</p>
        <p>Class B.—Privates Gilbert, Wallington, Bow- den, Moores, Barton, Walker, Battersby, PaoeY, E. Jones, Roe, Lewis, Hollows, Lamb, and Ditchfield.</p>
        <p>Class C.—Sergeant Newns, Corporal Cook, Privates E. Newns, F. Flood and Dunne. Class D.—Corporal Roberts and Private H. Jones.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>AN EXPEDITED WEDDING.</head>
        <p>AN EXPEDITED WEDDING.</p>
        <p>Private Battersby, who was t—o—li—al; been mar- ried to-day, arranged with his bride-elect, upon learning that be was to proceed to Devonport, to be married on Monday morning at Trinity Church. A few hours later the bridegroom started for the South, the bride and the wedding party beink at the Station to give him a hearty send-off.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>TO JOIN THE R.A.M.C.</head>
        <p>TO JOIN THE R.A.M.C.</p>
        <p>Thirty-three Voluntary Aid Detach- ment Volunteers. A special meeting of the Headquarters Divi- sion of the Warrington Corps of the St. John</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>IN THE COURTS.</head>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>SCENE IN A PASSAGE,</p>
        <p>A Territorial's Farewell to Ills Sweet-</p>
        <p>heart.</p>
        <p>ASSAULT CH iIIGE DISMISSED.</p>
        <p>When William Clark, 9, Louis-street, was saying '"good-bye" to his sweetheart, prior to his leaving with the Territorials for camp, his conduct was evidently misconstrued, for, at the Police court on Monday, the charge against him of assaulting Hugh McDermott, fruiterer, 49. Bewsey-street—who challenged him as to his presence along with a lady in a certain thor- oughfare—was dismissed. • Mr. A. Browne appeared for the prosecutor, and Mr. Swallow defended. •Mr. Browne said the prosecutor was a whole- sale fruiterer in the Market, and had shops in different parts of the town. There was a back passage going .out of Brown-street leading into Edgeworth-street, opposite Wycliffe Church, which had always been a nuisance to the in- habitants of the neighbourhood, owing to couples having been found there. On Saturday night about 12 o'clock Mr. McDermott left the Market with his three lady assistants and his son, Fred, for his home, where it was his cus- tom to " settle up." When they went up this back passage, which was a near cut to their home, they saw a couple in a corner. Mr. McDermott said to the man: "You have no business here at this time of night, and if you do not get 'out, down the passage, I shall fetch a policeman." He (Mr. Browne) might say there that not only Mr. McDermott, but many others, had appealed that a lamp should be erected at that corner. Prisoner then said: "Fetch a Policeman, I am with the most decent girl in Warrington. Do you pay for the ground?" Mr. McDermott said: :-` No; I only pay for a share of it." The girl ran away and prisoner put himself into a fighting attitude and struck at Mr. McDermott, but missed him. .Mr. McDermott put down the three overcoats and the money bag he was carrying, whereupon —it was more than a repetition of the Carpen- tier-Gunboat Smith fight—prisoner struck him in the eye whilst he was bending. Assisted by two of the girls, the man was conveyed into his house. The son, Fred, chased the prisoner down Brown-street, round by "The Examiner" Office, down Queen-street, and into the Market, where prisoner hid himself behind a skip, hay- -‘ big said to a man: "Don't let on." He was found by the skip by P.C. Taylor. When charged at the Police Station, prisoner said: "I did not hit him. I only pushed him in self- defence." A man did not run away after he had struck another man in self-defence, and hide behind a skip. Prisoner had said that no one had a right,to interfere with, his young lady and himself, and also that they had only been there five minutes. The prisoner or the lady did not live anywhere near to the passage. Mr. McDermott would tell them that what was happening was very disgraceful in the presence of the young girls. Mr. McDermott was attended by Dr. Langdale, who put four stitches over his eye. It was a most unprovoked assault and no question of self-defence could enter into the matter. With his forehead in bandages the complain- ant corroborated Mr. Browne's story, and said that prisoner struck, at him first, but missed, and then hit him in the eye. Evidence as to prosecutor's statement was given by the three female assistants and young Fred McDermott, and all denied, when cross- examined by Mr. Swallow. that the prosecutor made an improper suggestion to the prisoner. Mr. Swallow said it would be useless to, deny 'that complainant had . been injured, but his client .resented the suggestion that the com- plainant had made. Prisoner was a Territorial and was saying "Good-bye" to his sweetheart before leaving on the following day.for.camp.</p>
        <p>'Mr. McDermott made a remark to the defend- ant, Who resented it. Mr. McDermott struck the first blow, and his client simply pushed him and he fell, and in some way the injury was caused by the fall. Prisoner said he was a farrier in the Terri- torials, and that his sweetheart was employed at an hotel in the town. He met her after closing time and was saying "Good-bye" to her before going to camp. She was close to her home when the complainant came on the scene. By Mr. Swallow: He had his arm round the girl's twaist.. The complainant told him to "clear out," and witness asked him if the place belonged to him. He told McDermott to be careful what he said, then complainant struck him, first with his right hand and then with his left. He was about to strike again, when witness put his hand out to avoid the blow and complainant fell to the floor. He did not strike him in the eye. The lady, who was with prisoner. gave evi- dence in support of prisoner's statement, and, after retiring, the Bench unanimously dedided to dismiss the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. R. T. Fairclough (in the chair), Alderman Tinnion, Messrs. 'f. Pemberton and A. J. Jolley. "A Vile Bruto."—John William Lomax, 5, Victoria-street, was summoned for assault by his wife, Catherine.—Complainant said that on Thursday her husband was drunk, and because . .</p>
        <p>his dinner was not ready he started "carrying on." One word led to another, and he de- manded money, jumping up and thumping her in the eye. She did not wish to press the case, but would like a separation order. Her hus- band was very violent when in drink. She had had to be attended at the Infirmary for the injured eye.—lnspector Stuart said_that when he arrested defendant the pzevious night he was drunk in bed, and admitted the charge._ Defendant said he was very sorry.—The Chief Constable: He is a vile brute to his wife. I think he ought to be convicted of an aggra- vated assault and enable the wife to get a separation order. It is a monstrous thing that a man should go home in this state and beat his wife. She was in a state of collapse when she came to the Police Station, and had lost a good deal of blood.—Lomax was sent to prison , for one month.</p>
        <p>MONDAY. Before Dr. Joseph (in the chair), Aldermen Bolton and James Evans; Messrs. W. Furness, W. Roberts, and C. S. Burgess.</p>
        <p>A Girl in Trouble.—May Oarlin, who had previously been found wandering, was again</p>
