      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <p>before the magistrates, and in consequence of what the Chief Constable said in regard to the state of her mind, she was committed to the Workhouse on a fourteen days' order.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>TUESDAY.</head>
        <p>TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair), Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes and T. Locker.</p>
        <p>A Defaulting Husband.—Patrick Gill, lab- ourer, 92,. Hoyle-street, Warrington, was sum- moned by his wife, Agnes Gill, 3, Elizabeth- street, for arrears of maintenance and costs amounting-to £2 19s.—The Chief,Constable said the prisoner visited the Police Office on Mon- day night and intimated that he had been on the sick list and unable to work, but his (Mr. Nichol's) candid opinion was that prisoner did not intend to pay if he could get out of it. He was living with his aunt, and did not care a halfpenny for his wife, to whom he had not contributed anything for eleven weeks. If prisoner was ordered to a month probably the money would be fortheoniing.—Accused handed a doctor's note to the Bench. He was willing . .</p>
        <p>to pay. Ile was in receipt of 21s. per week. He was ordered to pay, or, in default, was com- mitted to prison for a month.—Accused I cannot pay it.</p>
        <p>Separation Orders Granted.—A separation order was made against Walter Dakin, tanner, 9, Lower Wash-lane, Warrington, on the appli- cation of his wife, Elizabeth, who, in asking for 10s. peF week as alimony, alleged that her husband was an habitual drunkard. He did not deny this and offered her 88. per week. He was out of work. An order for Bs. per week was made, the Chairman intimating that if the defendant secured a, good position the Court would consider increasing the amount.—ln a second case John Griffin, 32, James-street, was ordered to pay his wife, Ellen, 10s. per week. The application was made on the grounds of desertion The wife stated that the defendant had turned her out of the house. He was con- tinually ill-treating her and never seemed to be sober.—The Chief Constable stated that was defendant's 55th appearance. \</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>THURSDAY.</head>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Mr. A. J. Jolley (in the chair), Dr. .Naden, and Mr. W. Roberts. Anxious to Join their Regiments.—James Toole, or Devine, or Jenkins, 14, Platt-sereet, Warrington, was charged with being a deserter from the Ist Battalion East Lancashire Regi- ment, stationed at Woking, since August 18th, 1910. Superintendent Williamson said the prisoner, who surrendered the previous day, was very anxious to go back to his regiment. He was ordered to be hand4d to the military authorities.—Bernard Johnson, no settled abode, was charged with being a deserter from the 2nd. Battalion South Lancashire Regiment, stationed at Curragh, Kildare, since May 23, 1913. Super- intendent Williamson stated that this prisoner also surrendered and was anxious to return to his regiment. A corporal from Orford Barracks was in attendance and prisoner was handed over to his charge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.-</head>
        <p>COUNTY PETTY SESSIONS.-</p>
        <p>WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>Before Messrs. F. Aylmer Frost and J. Ball. Without a Licence.—John Thomas Crompton, motorclriver, 32, Sandhurst-street, Latchford, was summoned for driving a motor car without having a licence.—He pleaded guilty.—The police evidence showed that when defendant was asked for his licence in Liverpool-road, Sankey, on July 20, he stated he had changed his jacket before he came out and had left the licence be- hind. Inquiries were made and it was found that the licence had expired.—Defendant said he had been off work for three weeks, and the licence had expired during that period.—De- fendant was fined 2s. 6d. including costs.</p>
        <p>Factory Acts Offences.—James Roberts, of Fearnhead, was charged on two counts with em- ploying young persons under 16 years of age.for more than seven working days without obtain- ing a medical certificate. The defendant was also charged with failing to limewash his prem- ises within 14 months of the previous limewash, and further with failing to enter in a register particular- as to the whitewashing of the fac- tory.—On the application of Mr. H. E. Brothers (H.M. Inspector of Factories), who prosecuted. the last charge was withdrawn on the defendant promising to pay the costs.—The Chairman stated that defendant would be fined ,R 1 and costs in the first two cases and £5 and costs in the third case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>WILL IT LAST? Widnes Case.</head>
        <p>WILL IT LAST? Widnes Case.</p>
        <p>"But will my cure last?" That is the anxious question of the unhappy kidney sufferer, and the assuring word of this Widnes resident, who has stayed cured for twelve months, will be glad news indeed. _</p>
        <p>On January 13th, 1913, Mrs. R. Dennett, of 12, Charles-street, Simra's Cross, Widnes, said: "I was subject to kidney complaint for several years. I have had a large famil);, and have no doubt this has weakened my kidneys. I suffered from terrible pains in the small of my back, which were often, so severe that I would have to cry out. 'When I stooped the pains were like knives being driven into ray back. I was weary and tired, and my work was a burden to me. • The pains in my head nearly drove me out of my mind. "The urinary system was out of order, there was a constant desire for relief, and yet the water was painful and scanty and full of a dark, heavy sediment. " Through reading-about a similar case being cured by Doan's Backache Kidney Pills I thought I would try them. To my delight they soon began to put me right. Two boxes of this excellent medicine cured me and improved ray general health. I have used them since then, and they always do me good. "One time the shopman sent some other pills, which I tried, but I might as well have throWn my money away, for they did me no good. Now I always stick to Doan's Pills, as I have proved their great value by experience. I have widely recommended them amongst my friends. (Signed) R. Dennett."</p>
        <p>On November 26th, 1913—NEARLY TWELVE MONTHS LATER—Mrs. Dennett said: "My cure by Doan's Pills has been a lasting one, and my health is now splendid." Price 2/9 a box, 6 boxes 13/9; of all dealers, or from Foster-McClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W. Don't ask for back- ache or kidney pills—ask DISTINCTLY for DOAN'S Backache Kidney Pills, the same as Mrs: Den- nett had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>CITY OF GUILDS OF LONDON INSTITUTE. Examination Results.</head>
        <p>CITY OF GUILDS OF LONDON INSTITUTE. Examination Results.</p>
        <p>Technical Institute.</p>
        <p>The following are the results of examinations up to date:— Mechanical Engineering (Grade I.).—Pass: Harold Carter, Charles Challoner. Mechanical Engineering (Grade ll.).—Second class: Harold Carter, Fred Bennett, Alfred Light; Robert Eden. Heating and Ventilation (grade I.).—Pass: Stanley C. Appleton, Ralph Acton. Carpentry and Joinery (Grade ll.).—First class: John L. Collins. Second class :4,Tohn Henshaw.</p>
        <p>Principles of Leadwork (Final).—Second class: Stanley C. Appleton, Albert Hughes, Harold V. Lea.</p>
        <p>Builders' Quantities (Grade I.).—First class : William S. Jerams. Second class: John L. Col- lins; Douglas bolt.</p>
        <p>Brickwork (Grade I.).—Second class: John Lea.</p>
        <p>Magnetism and Electricity (External).—Second class : Frederick- J. Gore. _</p>
        <p>_ Manual Training, (Woodwork).—First year, pass: Frederick Bennett, Jack Whitehouse, Prank Knowles. Final, second class: Maurice Glover.</p>
        <p>School of Art. Cabinet Making (Gra(le I.).—Second class: Fred Bennett. Embroidery (Final).—Second class: Mildred Lowe. Embroidery (Grade I.).—Second class: Sophie Clatworthy, Mildr.. Shakoshaft. Painters' and Decorators' Work, (Final).— Second class: Herbert IL Dell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>THE;</head>
        <p>THE;</p>
        <p>ER, -SATURDAY,</p>
        <p>t-` I, DISTRICT cif itws 11</p>
        <p>STOeKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Choir.—The annual picnic which is provided by the truScees of the church took place on Bank Holidikv to Llangollen. Leaving Stockton Heath at 6.45 a.m. by special 'bus, the members of the choir, accompanied by ;),Ir. F. E. Penketh (choirmaster) and Mr. G. Whitfield (secretary of the Trust), who had arranged the picnic, proceeded to Bank Quay Station to join the 7.15 train to Llangollen. The party found much enjoyment in sailing up the canal to Berwyn, and visited the famous Horseshoe !Falls and Llantysilio Church, and in the after- noon, after a great effort, managed to climb Castle Dinas Brau, from the summit of which a splendid view of the EV.autiful valley of Llangollen was obtained. Tea Was served at the Eivion Hotel, to which full justice was clone. 'After the repast a visit was paid to Plas Newydd, the abode of the world-renowned "Maids of Llangollen." The return journey was commenced at 7.20. A thoroughly enjoy- able day was spent, the amateur photographer being much in evidence, as was also the vocal talents of the choir, who, on the pleasure boats, in the hotel, in the railway train, and on the station platform, drew crowds of listeners, who were charmed by the harmony of this village !choir.. On arrival at Bank Quay Station, the singing of "God bless Our native land" and the National Anthem, so fitting- in the time of national crisis, brought one of the most enjoy- able outings to a close.</p>
        <p>time through which we were passing we were so much occupied in efforts to relieve sorrow and misery that it was pleasant to think and speak upon some subject in which there was no ele- ment of anxiety or calamity, and which touched entirely upon the happy and peaceful side of life. Flowers and the lzve of Nature brought beauty before us, and true sympathy with . Nature could not fail to exercise a good moral influence. V,e were also living in a very material age, which tended to turn the whole trend of thought towards the love of luxury. Mere money-making might often be allowed to eclipse a great deal that was productive of true happiness and peace. However, far be it from her to undervalue the practical uses of horticul- ture. With regard to the exhibits from cottage gardens, she felt glad that every encouragement was being given to them, and entirely concurred with the wishes expressed by the committee that every effort should be made to enhance their appearance. When motoring in that neighbour- hood and in other parts of England, one was often struck by the delightful blaze of colour to be seen in many cottage gardens, which largely contributed towards the charm of an English landscape. She did not think one met with that in other countries. She noted with pleasure that members of that society exchanged plants freely.. 'This established the principle of give and take, and eliminated the selfish desire, which was rather inherent in human nature, for one man to keep the best for himself. She was glad to see that exhibits of poultry and eggs were in- cluded, although in a neighbourhood of that kind one could understand that it might be difficult for farmers to devote much time and labour to poultry-keeping and butter-making. She felt sure that every housewife would feel an interest in the display of table decorations. It was always a help to find some new ideas, for everyone was apt to be stereotyped. It seemed to be an excellent proposition that visits should be arranged for members to various gardens in the neighbourhood, and in conclusion she would say that in the near future her husband and she hoped to have the pleasure of welcoming them to Appleton. (Applause.) Miss Mabel Broady presented Mrs. Lyon with a beautiful bouquet of orchids, and carnations, amidst loud applause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>TIIE SHOW.</head>
        <p>TIIE SHOW.</p>
        <p>Increasvil interest in ihe Vegetable</p>
        <p>Classes.</p>
        <p>A RECORD ENTRY.</p>
        <p>Glorious weather favoured the Stockton Heath Garden Society's show, which was held at Wilderspool Park on Monday. There was an exceedingly large attendance of visitors, who greatly appreciated the beautiful display that was made. TheretWas an encouraging entry, for there were no less than 600 competitors, whereas last year. there were only about 400. The cot- tage gardens, which had been judged prior io the show, all looked at their best, although the recent heavy rains had marred their beauty to a slight extent. There was a marked increase in the interest taken in vegetables, &amp;c., and, on the whole, the show was highly successful. The public themselves acted as judges for the table decorations, and when the votes were counted Mrs. Stone bad received 315, which entitled her to the vase presented by Lady Greenall. Mrs. Broady was awarded the second prize. The judges, in conversation with an " Examiner " representative, expressed their appreciation of the show in general, and especially congratu- lated the vegetable growers. They were also unanimous in their opinion•as to the beautiful non-competitive exhibits by Mr. J. C. Parr, Mr. Robert Bolton (Carnforth), Messrs. Bees, Ltd. (Liverpool), and Messrs. Dickson, of Chester. A model aeroplane, as well as a hive of bees, the latter having been sent by Mr. W. Antrobus, of Walton Inferior, were amongst the special features of the show.</p>
        <p>In addition to the ordinary attractions, there were roundabouts, hoop-la stalls, and all the necessary "shows" which tend to the success of a fete. For the athletic sports, in Which there was much enthusiasmA and no small, degree of amusement, Mr. J. 13. Baxter acted; as starter, and Mr. Walter Howard was an in- defatigable secretary. The band of the R.A.0.8. played beautiful selections under the able con- ductorship of Mr. R. Smith. The Scarlet Poms, of whom Mr. W. W. Whittle is a most able leader, delighted the crowds by their witticisms and well-rendered songs. The members 'of this most entertaining group were: Leader, Mr. W. W. Whittle (humorist), Miss Elsie Hewitt (soprano), Messrs. Tom Crowe (alto), J. Sephton (tenor), W. Dutton (Ist bass), W. J. Hopkins (2nd bass), W. Morris (ventriloquist), and Albert Trow (pianist).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>OFFICIALS.</head>
        <p>OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>The officials of the society- are as follows: Presidgnt, Sir Gilbert Greenall; Mr. W. H Woodcock (chairman), Mr. J. H. Fletcher (vice- chairman). Committee: Messrs. F. Baker, J. B. Baxter, J. Bell, D. S. Birrell, F. Broady, F. Brook, J. Cannell, G. A. Dunlop, J. Ellison, J. F. Ferguson, R. Gorton, A. E. Harrison, H. B. Houghton. G. Houghton, J. McDermid, R. Mochrie, J. Morris, J. Owen, F. A. Rathbone, J. Richardson, W. Skelton, F. Timperley, E. Vernon, E. W. Whitehouse and A. S. Wright; Mr. Louis A. Voisey (hon. auditor), Mr. J. Holt (hon. treasurer), and Mr. E. G. White (hon. secretary). _</p>
        <p>The judges were: General classes, Mr. Joseph Stoney (Woolton), and Mr. G. Corbett (Altrinc- ham); gardens, Mr. R. Poulton; farm produce, Mr. Thomas Baxter.</p>
        <p>OPENING CEREMONY.</p>
        <p>Fortunately, the weather was beautiful at the opening ceremony, which was so gracefully per- formed by Mrs. Charles Lyon, of Appleton Hall. Sir Gilbert Greenall presided, and he was sup- ported by Lady Greenall, Colonel Lyon, Mrs. Parr, Mr. and Mrs. Vere B. Davies, Mrs. W. Bolton, Misses Broadbent, Messrs. W. Bain- bridge, H. R. Armitage, P. M. Oliver, R. I. Wynne Jones, H. Beresford 'Melville, J. If. Clarke, W. Skelton, W. H. Woodcock, F. Tim- nerley, T. Baxter, H. J. Westbrook, F. A. Jen- kins, J. McDermid, H. B. Houghton, J. H. Fletcher, and E. G. White (hon. secretary).,</p>
        <p>The Chairman said that letters of apology had been received from Lord Wolmer, the -Revs. J. Collins and H. V. Pigot,, Mrs. Milling, Miss Bessie Dutton, Mr. and Mrs. Ransome, Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield, and Mr. Alan Sykes. They would agree with him that it was Mr. Sykes' duty to be in his place in the. House of Commons in the very trying time through which the nation was passing. (Hear, hear.) Sir Gilbert continued, and said an inspection of the exhibits mugt convince everybody of its excellence. The show was much larger than in any previous year, and the entries were some 200 in advance of last year's. A great deal more interest than was formerly the case was dis- playeq in the show throughout the district, and there was reason to believe that the operations of the society were appreciated by those who lived in the area which it covered. He appealed to members to do their utmost to induce new- comers to the district to join the society and to take an active interest in its work. It took a great deal of money to run the show, as prize money and other sources of expenditure had to .be considered, and the officials felt that the public were under some obligations to help them to make ends meet. There had been an increase of the number of classes this year, the new sec- tions including butter, eggs, and dressed poul- try. He was sorry to say that Mese classes were not very well filled, but that was probably due to the fact that it was their first year, and every- thing must have a trial before its use and value were fully proved. The trade exhibits formed a very attractive feature, but he thought the offering of a challenge cup or some other induce. ment would add to the attractiveness of the dis- play. (Hear, hear.) The officials would do their best for the advancement of the show if the public would support them, as they were anxious that Stockton Heath show should stand higher than any other in this part of the world. (Hear, hear.) The recent rainy weather had been very unfavourable for specimen flowers, and he was sure visitors would readily excuse and forgive any deficiencirs on that account. The classes for vegetables were, however, exception- ally strong, there being no fewer than 20 entries in the section for garden peas. After thanking the donors of prizes for. their valuable help, Sir Gilbert said it was his pleasant duty to call upon Mrs. Lyon to open the show. Colonel and Mrs. Lyon were newcomers to the district. The Lyon ' family had, however, been associated with it for many years, and were highly respected and loved. He was sure that Colonel and Mrs. Lyon would carry out the fine traditions which Apple- ton had always handed dr. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>MRS. LYON'S SPEECH.</head>
        <p>MRS. LYON'S SPEECH.</p>
        <p>- - Mrs. Lyon said she felt much gratified at being asked to open the show. In the very strenuous</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>RELAXATION IN A TIME OF. CRISIS.</head>
        <p>RELAXATION IN A TIME OF. CRISIS.</p>
        <p>In proposing a vote of thanks to Mrs. Lyon, Mr. 11. R. Armitage said that no man could do justice to himself unless he had a hobby or some kind of relaxation to divert his mind from his ordinary occupation. If that were true in thp normal times of life, it was particularly true at a period of crisis like the present, when it was given to but few to be active, and to many to bear the harder though passive part of the work. In opening that show Mrs. Lyon had shown them how they could best take relaxation from their passive part in the events of this great crisis.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. H. Woodcock seconded, and said that they had all been charmed by Mrs. Lyon's ad- dress, which had emphasised many of the points brought before them by the work of the garden society. The show, which was this year on A larger scale than before, constituted only one portion of their activities. There were also the cottage gardens, the window boxes and the ex- hibits which were 0:own inside but not brought to the show. The society believed that in such a parish as Stockton Heath, which in itself was not especially beautiful, each garden could be made still more attractive and the district generally better provided with the amenities of life. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>A vote of,thanks was proposed to Sir Gilbert by Mr, P. M. Oliver, who saia fliat77'..e society did not rest content`with giving prizes and hold- ing shows; one of the best features—a feature which set it above most horticultural societies-- was the enormous amount of effort it devoted to encouraging small people with small gardens. After referring to the difficulties which beset the gardener, Mr. Oliver said he was recently going through a garden when the facetious owner, indicating one flourishing set of vegeta- bles, said, "Here are the beans." Then, point- ing to an empty bed, he added, "And there are the should-have-beens." (Laughter.) All who indulged in gardening knew of the " should- have-beens "—the flowers which ought to have won prizes, but which never did. (Laughter.) Before coming to that show he went to his garden to pluck a particular kind of rose which bloomed under his window the "Lady Greenan" rose. The variable weather—to which they could apply the words of Stevenson with regard to a little child : "When it's good, its very good, and when it's bad it's horrid " (laughter)—had, however, completely spoiled the " Lady Greenall " rose, but he was glad to find that it had not interfered with Lady Greenall's show. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>In seconding, Colonel Lyon said that although he was a newcomer to the district he had known Sir Gilbert Greenall a very long time and had had opportunities of seeing his work. When they thought of all Sir Gilbert's activities—the Stockton Heath show being only one very small item among the many and varied organisations which he encouraged—they ffiust feel that they should heartily congratulate themselves upon having him as their neighbour, and upon receiving his .help as president of that society. It showed that Sir Gilbert was doing his duty, and that, to one who had been a soldier and who had lived amongst soldiers all his life, was the highest praise that could be given to anybody. (Hear, hear.) Pulling together and a sense of duty were the things that led to success, and if they wanted to find true pulling together and a sense of duty they must go to the Army for it. (Hear, hear.) It is the first thing that is taught, and I think the practical reason for that, con- tinued Colonel Lyon; is that nothing draws people together more than the sense that the enemy's bullets are quite impartial and may hit any one of us. The consequence is that We are all exposed to the same dangers; there is no class distinction. The bullet may hit an officer or a drummer boy; there is no regard to class, and one forgets all about that sort of thing if one is a soldier. We are going into a very grave crisis in this country, At any moment a telegram may come ordering us to mobilise—or those of us who have the privilege of going. I hope that will bring home to the country what it means to pull together, and that all little differences will be forgotten. (Applause.) Sir Gilbert, in reply, said that he only hoped they would have a most enjoyable afternoon.</p>
        <p>OFFICIAL REPORTS., The report presented by Mr. R. Poulton with regard to the gardens was as follows :</p>
        <p>Class 1.-4 n this class, which is perhaps the most important from a practical point of view, much skill is shown in the cultivation of vege- tables, and as the vegetable section of the sum- mer show must greatly depend on the quality and quantity of produce contributed from these cottage gardens, every effort should be made to increase the number of entries.</p>
        <p>Class 2.—This class, which is well contested, includes some exrellent exhibits, and the bright display of flowers to be seen in the gardens, reflects great credit on the exhibitors, and also affords striking evidence of the good work the society is doing by encouraging the cultivation of flower gardens.</p>
        <p>Class 3.—This is an important class in which there is keen competition and some noteworthy exhibits.</p>
        <p>Class 4.—The exhibits here are all deserving of praise, and owing to the uniform character of the gardens they are usually difficult to judge. From the number of points awarded the competitors will note that there is• very little disparity amongst them.</p>
        <p>Class s.—Great credit is due to all the com- petitors in this class who have so successfully transformed their bare, unsightly back yards</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>JOHNSTON WATSON'S</head>
        <p>JOHNSTON WATSON'S</p>
        <p>Scientifically fitted GLASSES cure HEADACHES. SIGHT TESTING OPTICIAN,</p>
        <p>84, BRIDGE ST., Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>MCAbIIN</head>
        <p>MCAbIIN</p>
        <p>k_UGUST 8, 1914.</p>
        <p>into trim and neatly kept flower gardens. It is to be regretted that there are not more entries.</p>
        <p>Class 6.—Here again, as in Class 5, much . credit is due to the two' competitors, who in another way have turned their back yards to good account by the erection of small green- houses, which at the time of judging were well stocked with a bright collection of greenhouse plants.</p>
        <p>Class 7.—Although there is only one exhibit in this class, the competitor is well worthy of first prize for his well kept and well stocked greenhouse.</p>
        <p>Class B.—This is one of the most important classes in the oompetition, in which the exhi- bitors show much skill and good taste in their choice of flowers and laying ont their gardens. We have specimens here of rock garden, her- baceous garden, and formal bedding, in each case arranged with pleasing effect. No. 40 in this class which I recommend kr special prize is a very pretty rock garden, somewhat exten- sive, but I consider rather out of place here and would be better in Class 3. Class 9.—The three window boxes in this class are arranged with good taste and very credit- able to the exhibitors.</p>
        <p>Class 10.— IA this class some fine healthy creepers are shown, and are in most eases very effective. The young growths of these creepers grow very rapidly and are apt • to encroach oz,i the gutters and tiles of the dwelling—where they lose their usefulness and very often look untidy. Competitors should bear this in 'Rind. Class 11.—This class is a very needful one as nothing adds so much to the appearance of a garden as a well grown and well kept hedge, and all the exhibits in this class , are good examples in that respect.</p>
        <p>Class 12.—The popularity of the rose in the Stockton Heath district is apparent in this class, and judging from the excellent quality of the exhibits there are some expert rose growers amongst the exhibitors. The society is quite justified in introducing this special class.</p>
        <p>Class 13.—The exhibits here are all very nice, and show that a good deal of care and attention have been given to them.</p>
        <p>Class 14.:-Theseeiiil;its (small garden plots) are excellent and great praise is due to the competitors for the success they have obtained. It is a pity there are not more entries in this section for juniors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>PRIZE-WINNERS. Appended is the list of prize-winnns</head>
        <p>THE PRIZE-WINNERS. Appended is the list of prize-winnns</p>
        <p>Garden Section.</p>
        <p>The best kept and best stocked cottage gar- den.-1, E. Mills; 2, E. Taylor; 3, Joseph Miller; he, J. Spring and F. Kippax. The neatest and best arranged garden in any position.-1, Joseph Morris; 2, W. Melling; 3, J. Bretherton ; he, P. Cliffe and G. Fleet; c, J. Ellison, P. Starkey, J. Gleave, F. Jones and J. Houghton. _ _</p>
        <p>The neatest and best arranged garden in any position-1, F. A. Rathbone; 2, J. Blinston; 3, J. B. Baxter; he, A. Owen, G. Houghton and Miss Greenhough. The neatest and best arranged small front garden.-1, J. Bennett; 2, J. Holt, 3, G. Leigh; he, H. Cross.</p>
        <p>The neatest and best stocked back yard garden.-1, F. Acton; 2, T. H.Oraithwaite; 3, T. Allen.</p>
        <p>The neatest, cleanest and best stocked back yard greenhouse.-1, J. Colley; 2, T. Williams.</p>
        <p>The neatest, cleanest and best stocked green- house in any other position.-1, D. S. Bin-ell. The neatest and best arranged villa garden 1, F. Broady; 2, A. Broady; 3, J. B. Baxter; hc, W. H, Woodcock and W. Skelton. The neatest and best arranged window box.- 1, J. Houghton; 2, J. Bennett; 3, T. H. Braith- waite.</p>
        <p>The best display of creepers on house, winner in 1913 not eligible.-1, Mr. Shackleton; 2, G. W. Clubley; 3, C. Young; he, J. Blinston and F. Act On.</p>
        <p>Best kept hedge.-1, E. Mills; 2, F. A. Rath- bone; 3, W. A. Timperley ; he, Mr. Shackleton. . Collection of rose trees in beds.-1, J. Blin- ston; 2;F. Broady; 3, J. B. Baxter; he, G. Boughton and F. A..Rathbone; e, P. Cliffe.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>Junior Section.</head>
        <p>Junior Section.</p>
        <p>Best display of plants in flower grown in a box.-1, Jack Gleave; 2, Bertha Langley; 3, Helga Acton. The neatest and best stocked small garden plot.-1, Donald Birrell; 2, Stanley Birrell; 3, Bertha Langley and Freda Bailey.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>Window Plants.</head>
        <p>Window Plants.</p>
        <p>One begonia in bloom: 1, J. Gleave; 2, E. Taylor; 3, H. Harrop. One fuchsia (double or single) in bloom: 1, E. R. Worrall; 2, J. Gleave; 3, F. Langley. One geranium in bloom.: 1, F. Langley; 2, E. Taylor; 3, J. Gleave. One aspidistra: 1, Mrs. A. Lockett; 2, Mrs. J. Holt; 3, J. Gleave. One fern: 1, E. Taylor; 2, E. B. Worrall; 3, F. Langley. One campanula, white or blue, in bloom: 1, B. W. Woodward; 2, A. Wilkinson; 3, E. Taylor. One plant, any other variety, in bloom: 1, E. Mills; 3, E. Taylor. One foliage plant, any other variety: 1, H. Harrop.</p>
        <p>Greenhouse Plants.</p>
        <p>Four plants, two in flower and two foliage: 1, F. Acton; 2, J. Colley; 3, D. S. Birrell. Two ferns: 1, F. Acton; 2, C. Young. Two begonias in bloom: 1, J. Bark ; 2, D. S. Birrell; 3, P. Acton. Two fuchsias (double or single) in bloom: 1, D. S. Birrell; 2,.J. Colley; 3, J. Bark. Two geraniums (double or single) in bloom: 1, F. Kippax; 2, J. Colley; 3, D. S. Birrell. Two ivy-leaved geraniums (in bloom): 1, D. S. Birrell; 2, F. Kippax. Two foliage plants: 1, Mrs. Stone; 2, D. S. Birrell; 3, F. Acton. Any two plants in bloom: 1, F. Acton; 2, A Owen; 3, D. S. Birrell.</p>
        <p>Cut Blooms. In-door grown.-Basket, vase, or bowl of cut blooms, one or more varieties: a 2 , D. S. Birrell. Out-door grown.-Three vases of sweet peas, mixed, not to exceed 20 sprays in each vase, arranged for effect: 1, F. Broady; 2, D. S. Bir- rell; 3, E. Taylor. Four vases of sweet peas, dis- tinct varieties, 12 sprays in each vase, foliage, not allowed: 1. H. L. Williams; 2, E. Taylor,; 3, A. J. Mottram, Nine vases of .sweet pettil, dis- tinct varieties; 12 sprays in each vase: 1, D. S. Birrell; 2, A. J. Mottram. Eight sprays of white sweet peas, one variety only: 1, H. L. Williams; 2, E. Taylor; 3, J. H. Fletcher. Eight sprays of blush or pink sweet peas, one variety only: 1, E. Taylor; 2, T. Williams; 3, S. J. Woodcock. Eight sprays of mauve or lavender sweet peas, one variety only: 1, E.• Taylor; 2, T. Williams; 3, H. L. Williams. Eight sprays of crimson or scarlet sweet peas, one variety only: 1, E. ,Taylor; 2, J. Owen; 3, H. L. Williams. Eight sprays of any one variety of sweet peas: 1, W. Rov,-bottorn; 2, J. H. Fletcher; 3, P. Williams. Twelve sprays of mixed varie- ties of sweet peas: 1, J. A. Fletcher; 2, T. Williaths; 3, H. L. Williams. For the best pot, tub or box of growing sweet peas: 1, D. S. Birrell. ,Twelve pansies, show or fancy, to be shown with collars: 1, W. A. Timperley; 2, A. J. Mottram; 3, E. W. Whitehouse. Six asters: 1, A. J. Mottram; 2, P. Cliffe ; 3, E. W. White- house. Six stocks: 1, E. W. Whitehouse; 2, W. Rosebottom; 3, A. J. Mottram. Six dahlias, any variety: 1, F. Kippax; 2, E. Taylor; 3, E. W. Whitehouse. Six roses, distinct varieties to be named: 1, F. Broady; 2, J. B. Baxter; 3, A. J. Mottram; he, J. Owen. Twelve roses, to be named: 1, F. Broady; 2, A. J. Mottram. Bowl or vase of cut roses, own foliage, arranged for effect: 1, B. Baxter; 2, E. W. Whitehouse; 3, J. Owen. The best single spray of any variety of rose, in a plain glass vase: 1, G. Houghton; 2, J. Owen; 3, J. B. Baxter. Bowl or vase of cut roses: 1, J. B. Baxter; 2, J. A. Rathbone; 3, P. Cliffe. Bowl or vase of cut rambler roses, any variety: 1, J. B. Baxter; 2, J. A. Rath- bone; 3, J. Blinston. Six carnations or picotees: 1; J. F. Fergifson; 2, P. Cliffe; 3, E. W. White- house. One vase of carnations or Ipicotees : 1, E. W. Whitehouse; 2, A. J. Mottram; 3, J. A. Rathbone. Eighteen violas, in three varieties: 1, W. A. Timperley; 2, E. W. Whitehouse; 3, A. J. Mottram. Nine violas, in three varieties: 1, W. A. Timperley; 2, E. W. Whitehouse; 3, A. J. Mottram. Collection of cut blooms, not to exceed twelve varieties, to be arranged in space 4ft. by 2ft. 6in.: 1, F. Kippax. Collection of four vases of cut blooms, distinct species: 1, E. W. Whitehouse; 2, A. E. Taylor; 3, A. J. Mottram. One vase of one variety cut bloom, not otherwise classed: 1, A. J. Mottram; 2, F. Langley; 3, F. Kippax. Two asters: 1, H. Mottram; 2, W. Lockett; 3, IL Harrop. Two stocks: 1, G. Ifooikr; 2, H. Harrop; 3, W. Rosebottom. Two carnations: 1, J. Gleave; 2, W. Lockett; 3, J. Colley. Two roses: 1, J. A. Rathbone; 2, H. Harrop; 3, F. Langley. Two dahlias (any variety): 1, W. Lockett; 2, 11. Harrop; 3, H. Mottram. Two perennial phlox: 1, H. Harrop; 2, E. Taylor; 3, F. Kippax. Twelve violas: 1, W. A. Tituperley; 2, E. W. White- house; 3, J. B. Baxter. Vase of violas arranged for effect: 1, J. B. Baxter; 2, IL Harrop; 3, B.</p>
        <p>W. Whitehouse. Collection of three vases of cut blooms: 1, H. Harrop; 3, A. J. Mottram. Vase of any one variety of flower: 1, E. W. Whitehouse; 2, G. Hooper; 3, Miss A. M. Hal- lows. Vase of mixed flowers: 1, W. Rosebottom ; 2, Mrs. Hargreaves; 3, E. W. Whitehouse.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>Junior Section.</head>
        <p>Junior Section.</p>
        <p>Collection of wild flowers, not exceeding 12 varieties, for members under 12 years of age: 1, J. Dunlop; 2, Nellie Douglas; 3, Helga Acton; he, Ethel Walker. Collection, not exceeding 20 varieties, for members 12 years of age and over: 1, M. Broady; 2, Minnie Ffankey ; 3, Freda Bailey. Vasesof. wild flowers arranged for effect, for members under 12 years of age: 1, Helga Acton; 2, J. Bretherton ; 3, Nellie Douglas. Vase for members 12 years of age and over: 1, Cissie Buck; 2, Dorothy Mills; 3, Lillie Byfleld ; he, M. Broady. Best arrangement of glasses in vase: 1, Freda Bailey; 2, M. Broady; 3, E. Snape; he, Helga Acton and Lillie Byfield.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>Fruit and Vegetables.</head>
        <p>Fruit and Vegetables.</p>
        <p>For members only.-One dish of apples : 1, A. Broady; 2. J. Starkey; 3, S. J. Woodcock; he, J. Spring. Twelve shallots: 1, J. Cooper; 2, E. Mills; 3, 11. L. Williams. Six onions: 1, Mrs. Stone; 2, J. Miller; 3, E. Mills; he, G. Green. Dish of peas: 1, S. Starkey; 2, J. Starkey; 3, E. Taylor; he, 0. Cooper. Dish of runner beans: 1, J. Starkey; 2, S. Starkey; 3, G. Cooper. Dish of drawf beans: 1, A. Owen; 2; E. Mills; 3, W. Byford. Dish ef broad beans: 1, G. Cooper; 2, E. Taylor; 3, E. Mills; he, H. L. Williams. One red cabbage: 1, W. Byford; 2, G. Green. One white cabbage: 1, J. Cooper; 2, A. Owen; 3, R. H. Drury; hc, E. Taylor. Two cos lettuce: 1, E. Taylor; 2, J. Spring; 3, E. Mills. Two cab- bage lettuce: 1, A. Owen; 2, G. Green; 3, E. Tay- lor. Three sticks celery: 1, A. Owen; 2, G. Green; 3, E. Taylor. Two dishes of two varieties of potatoes: 1, H. L. Williams; 2, E. Mills; 3, E. Taylor; he, J. Miller. Six carrots: 1, A. Owen; 2, B. W. Woodward; 3, G. Cooper; he, W. By- ford. Five tomatoes: 1, J. Owen; 2, A. Broady; 3, D. S. Birrell. One vegetable marrow: 1, J. Miller; 2, W. A. Timperley ; 3, E. Taylor. Two cauliflowers: 1, S. Starkey; 2, J. Starkey; 3, E. Mills. Six white turnips: 1, E. Taylor; 2, F. Kippax ;3, J. Hallows. Six leeks: 1, F. Kippax ; 2, H. L. Williams; 3, A. Owen. Three beet- globe: 1, S. Miller; 2, Mrs. A. Lockett; 3; J. Hallows. Six parsnips: 1, B. W. Woodward; 2, A. Owen; 3, J. Spring. Three beet-long: 1, A. Broady; 2, E. Mills; 3, E. Taylor. Six sticks of rhubarb: 1, J. Miller; 2, E. Taylor; 3, E. Mills. Collection of vegetables-six kinds, excluding tomatoes and cucumbers: 1, E. Taylor; 2, G. Green; 3, A. Owen.</p>
        <p>Open to all.-Twelve roses, not less than six varieties : 1, J. Moores; 2, J. Blinston. Six roses, not less than three varieties : 1, J. Apple- ton.; 2, J. Green; 3, G. Carmen. Collection of cut blooms, not to exceed twelve varieties, to be arranged in space 4ft. by 2ft. 6in : 1, J. Hamp- son. Four vases sweet peas: 1, J. Pye. Six plants in bloom: 1, D. S. Birrell; 2, J. Hampson. Collection of vegetables, six kinds, excluding tomatoes and cucumbers: 1, J. Carmen; 2, J. Kippax ; 3, E. Mills. Dish of peas: 1, J. Bing- ham; 2, E. Taylor; 3, S. Starkey. Dish of toma- toes: 1, J. Hampson; 2, A. Broady; 3, J. Bing- ham. Two vegetable marrows: 1, J. Miller; 2, J. Hampson; 3, J. Starkey. • Six vases of garden flowers: 1, J. Kippas ; 2, E. W. Whitehouse; 3, J. Hampson. The best decorated dinner table: 1, Mrs. Stone (315 votes); 2, Mrs. F. Broady (232 votes); 3, Miss Woodcock (147 votes); 4, Mrs. Hargreaves (80 votes). The most effective dis- play in hanging basket: 1, D. S. Birrell. " The Warrington Guardian " special prize for one vase of sweet peas, to consist of not more than 20 sprays in one or more varieties: 1, J. Owen ; hc, H. L. Williams. Gift class--Best exhibit of any variety pot plant, fern, cut bloom, fruit or vegetable: 1, E. G. White; 2, Bessie Baker; 3, E. Taylor. Three varieties potatoes: 1, T. Broady; 2, J. Miller; 3, J. Carmen; he, G. Car- men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>Poultry, Eggs, Butter, &amp;c,</head>
        <p>Poultry, Eggs, Butter, &amp;c,</p>
        <p>Dish of six hen eggs, brown: 1, J. Owen; 2, Mrs. Hankey; 3, Mrs. T. Broady. Dish of six hen eggs, white: 1, S. Starkey; 2, J. Owen; 3, Mrs. T. Broady. The best dressed fowl: 1, Mrs. Mulkey; 2, Mrs. T. Broady. Four half-pounds of butter (slightly salted): 1, Miss A. Fairhurst; 2, Mrs. Hanley; 3, Mrs. T. Broady.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>A Successful Student. —Mr. Frank Davies, second son of Mr. Thomas Davies, North View, Penketli, has distinguished himself in the 'examinations at the Leathersellers- Companies', Technical College, London, where he has been, a student for the past two years, by obtaining the "College Diploma" fol. examinations (theoretical and practical) in the manufacture of leather and chemistry of leather manufac- ture. He also succeeded in obtaining the fol- lowing :—Advanced heavy leather tanning: Ist class certificate and Ist prize (.£'2 25.); chem- istry of leather manufacture: Ist class certifi- cate and Ist prize (.62 25.); dyeing and finishing I leather: Ist class certificate and half 2nd prize (10s: 6d.); light leather manufacture: Ist class I certificate. In the City and Guilds of London examinations, which are open to the British Isles, Mr. Davies won a bronze medal, and second prize of .61 10s. for the examination in the dressing of skins, and a second class certifi- , cate for dyeing of leather.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Wesleyan Presentation.—The members and friends of the Wesleyan Church met in the Guild Room on Tuesday evening to make a ' presentation to the Rev. E. and Mrs. Morgan, 1 who are leaving Padgate. Mr. L. Bennett, Mr. Duckworth and others spoke of Mr. Morgan's good work during his three years' residence in Padgate, especially amongst the young people, and said that the good influence he had exer- cised in connection with the Men's Class would long be remembered. They were sure that all wished them much happiness in their retire- ment.—Mr. L. Bennett then handed to Mr. Mor- gan a purse of gold.—Mr. Morgan thanked all for their good wishes, and said how he had enjoyed being at Padgate, and that he would always have very pleasant memories of the friends he was leaving behind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>TH EL WALL.</head>
        <p>TH EL WALL.</p>
        <p>THE PARISH SPORTS.</p>
        <p>The annual parish sports were held on Bank Holiday. Prior to the sports the children marched round the village in procession. Re- sults:—</p>
        <p>50, yards marble and spoon race for ladies: 1, Miss Kite; 2 Mrs. Morris Kite:• 50 yards .Whistling race for ladies and gentle- men: 1, Mr. Weston; 2, Mr. Percy Kite. 50 yards flat race for ladies': 1 Miss Lily Stubbs; 2, Miss Higginson. 100 yards witeeling wheelbarrow blindfolded:' Mr. Morris Kite.</p>
        <p>50 yards threading-needle race for ladies: 1, Mrs. Morris Kite; 2, Mrs. Bertha Walker. 100 yards flat race for men: 1, Mr. Sutton; 2, Ratenseroft.</p>
        <p>High iiimi)—f-ci";;3;n : Mr. P. A. Kite (4ft. 10in). 50 yards sack race: 1, Mr. J. Swinton; 2, Mr. R. liavenscroft ; 3, Mr. P. •Kite. 100 yards flat race: 1, Mr. R. Ravenscroft; 2, Mr. Sutton; 3, Mr. C. Ravenscroft. Tug-of-war (5 each side): Mr. R. Ravenscroft's team.</p>
        <p>The threading-the-needle race for ladies, and wheeling a wheelbarrow blindfolded contests created much amusement. Especial' interest was taken in the high jump and tug-of-war, and each event was well contested. The prizes were distributed by Mrs. Tiarks.</p>
      </div>
