      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>LOST BY ONR RUN. Warrington First Team'sjlard Luck on Saturday.</p>
        <p>Warrington were most unfortunate at Wigan on Satufday, when they received a reverse by 85-86. For the visitors, J. Thompson obtained the highest score, being one short of two dozen when he was caught. S. Partridge and B. Silcock were the only other double-figure scorers on the Warrington side, and they had respectively compiled 18 and 12 before they were caught from deliveries by Fairclough, who secured nine wickets at a cost of 27 runs. With the exception of two of the Wigan batsmen all were unable to reach double figures. R. M: Pucy wad W. A. Silcock were responsible for 47 runs between them, the former player claim- ing 26. "Extras" reached 14, and probably with a little more smartness in the field victory would have smiled on the Warrington men. B. Silcock's bowling was responsible. for 45 runs being made, but this was at the severe cost to the home team of seven wickets. W. Bennett took one wicket for eight runs, and Hannan' one for 17.</p>
        <p>On Monday Barrow paid a visit to the Arpley Ground, and failed to reach half the score that Warrington compiled. The chief scorer of the day was W. V. Turton, who was caught at 29— not a bad contribution to a total of 72. There was only one man in the visitors' eleven to reach double figures—and he only just, for when ten had been signalled to his credit he was caught by Holbrook. Barrow's aggregate reached 35.</p>
        <p>Rain was responsible for the non-completion of the match at 'Arpley on Saturday afternoon between the second elevens of Warrington and Wigan. The home players batted first, and chiefly through the energies of T. Williamson (45) and 11. Partridge (30) they carried the score to 123. Wigan had lost one wicket with their score at 29, when it was decided to abandon the match.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>RYLANDS' DECISIVE VVJN.</head>
        <p>RYLANDS' DECISIVE VVJN.</p>
        <p>Rylands' Recreation, in their Warrington and District League encounter with Walker's Brewery at Gorsey-lane on Saturday, secured a decisive victory, disposing of the opposition for a total of tour runs. The visitors occupied the crease in the first instance, but Could not with- stand the bowling-of McGowan and Carter, who divide&amp;the wickets. Only a single run was put up against McGowan, the remaining three being placed against the other victorious bowler. Rylands' compiled two short of 70 for the loss of eight wickets, Bramball, with 32, being the top scorer. On August Monday, at Gorsey-lane, Rylands' Recreation Second Eleven defeated the Cairo- street Unitarians after' an interesting match. The home team obtained 39 runs, to which J. Tracey contributed eleven—the highest score of the day—and in their reply the Unitarians milstered 25, the chief scorer to which +was A. Folbes, with eight not out. For Rylands', Wadsworth (four for 10 runs) and Richardson (five, for 13) were responsible for the downfall of the visitors, whose best bowlers proved to be Ellsrnore (six for 14) and Mather (three for 15).</p>
      </div>
