      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>INFANTILE MORTALITY.</head>
        <p>INFANTILE MORTALITY.</p>
        <p>Recommendations from the National Conference.</p>
        <p>Alderman James Evans, Councillors Broad- hurst and Hawthorn, and Dr. G. W. N. Joseph, medical officer of health, reporting as delegates to the National Conference on Infant Mortality, held last month in Liverpool, state that the progress of infant welfare work and the spread- ing of the principles of mothercraft were dis- cussed.</p>
        <p>The following resolutions were passed : (1.) "That this conference respectfully urges the President of the Local Government Board to take into consideration the advisability of making provision to secure early knowledge by 'Public Health Authorities of all still-births." /(2.) "That steps be taken to encourage the more general education of girls in matters relat- ing to mothercraft." (3.) "That this Conference urges that any further grants in aid of maternity should pro- vide in the first place for mothers now outside the operation of the maternity benefit under the Insurance Act, and in the second place for ade- quate care for pre-natal conditions, and that in the administration of the fund means should be taken to receive adequate return for the money spent." (4.) " That the President of the Local Gov- ernment Board should be requested to make the Notification of Births Act compulsory through- out the kingdom." (5.) "That the time has come for carrying on a general campaign throughout the country with the object of further reducing infant mor- tality and securing the better care of the matter before and after the birth of the child, and that the executive committee of this association be requested to take the necessary steps to this end." (6.) "That in th 6 opinion of this Conference any grant from the Exchequer in aid of Schools for Mothers, Babies Welcomes and similar organisations should be administered under the Local Government Board through the Local Sanitary Authority in order that administrative overlapping be avoided." The final resolution was passed by the War- rington Health Committee and adopted by the Council some weeks ago.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Grants for Child Welfare.</p>
        <p>The Monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural District Council was held in Bewsey Chambers on Wednesday, Councillor Longton (vice-chairman) presiding. There was an interesting discussion with reference to a memorandum from the Local Government Board on the proposed grant in aid Of provision by local authorities in, respect of maternity and child welfare. The doctor read the statement and said it would be rather expensive. He did not think it would do much good in a district like theirs. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell said the memoran- dum was very interesting and important. The Chairman: Do you think women would submit to all this nonsense? Mothers won't. The Doctor: No. The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: I don't think it is nonsense.</p>
        <p>On the proposition of Councillor Mather it was decided that copies of the memorandum he sent to each councillor.</p>
        <p>BURTONWOOD ROADS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell asked if anything had been done with regard to the roads at ,Burtonwood. The matter was very serious. The Surveyor: I have examined the roads and found that the damage occasioned by the diversion of traffic in consequence of the Sankey Bridge works, was so serious that the roads required special attention. After further discussion it was decided to co-operate with a neighbouring local body to ask the County Council to strengthen the road, or make a substantial grant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>The Latest Way With Tramps.</p>
        <p>Last week's record of the conviction, at the Warrington Borough Police Court, of a persist- ent and apparently 'incorrigible beggar suggests a timely allusion to the latest scheme whereby it is hoped such pests may be left without excuse for preying on society at large by means of false representation. Boards of Guardians in a given area unite in contribution to a central fund for the maintenance of stations midway between fairly distant unions where deserving " road- sters " may obtain a meal of bread and cheese, , and professional mendicancy may be detected, discountenanced and eventually suppressed. To be quite accurate, discrimination rests rather with experienced officials in charge of casual wards at the workhouses. It is generally left to the discretion of these, whose judgment sel- dom errs" where the more or less familiar " =tidier " is concerned, to detain Weary Willie until he has performa his task as of old, and to send the seemingly genuine and honest out-of-work on his way immediately after breakfast, so that he may make the most of the early morning in his search for employment. The estimated cost being unusually modest, so promising an experiment is surely worth while.</p>
        <p>The Unspeakable "Dummy." Coroner Brighouse's characteristic denuncia- tion of this: monstrous device for keeping troublesome infants quiet will be cordially echoed by all who have given any attention to the matter. The constant, use of the hateful thing often results, nbt only in permanent facial disfigurement, but to serious internal malforma- fion which involves heavy handicap through life. This is now so thoroughly understood by properly instructed and conscientious modern mothers that the " dummy " may be said to serve only to distinguish in an unenviable sense selfish parents who think more of personal ease and comfort than of the future good of their unfortunate offspring—just as some of them do not scruple to drug the innocent with poisonous compounds labelled "soothing syrup."</p>
      </div>
