        <p>CAPTAIN GEORGE CROSFIELD, Who served in South Africa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>EUROPEAN WAR.</head>
        <p>THE EUROPEAN WAR.</p>
        <p>This Map should be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>COUNTRIES SO FAR INVOLVED: 'eat Britain, Belgium, France, Russia, and So versus Germany and Austria-Hungary.</head>
        <p>COUNTRIES SO FAR INVOLVED: 'eat Britain, Belgium, France, Russia, and So versus Germany and Austria-Hungary.</p>
        <p>TERRITORIALS MOBILISED.</p>
        <p>Ready to Depart on the King's Service.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Territorials are now fully mobilised and are expected to leave Warrington to-day for service in some p,art of the country. The response has been excellent and the way the men have answered has given the greatest satisfaction to the officers.</p>
        <p>_ Warrington can now boast 1,000 Territorials amongst its citizens, and this strength has been attained through the loyalty of old soldiers, ex- Territorials, and ex-Volunteers, who, when they believed England was in need of men, came for- ward to answer the call. Since embodiment notices were issued, between 300 and 400 men have thrown in their la with the local batta- lion, and probably a number of these have done so in response to Lord Kitchener's appeal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>THE OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>_ The following are the officers of the battalion: Lieutenant-Colonel Fairclough, V.D.' Major Appleton, V.D., Major Crosfield, Captains R. Smith, Avery, Layton, Roberts, Cary, Monks, Egerton Fairclough and Woods, Lieutenants McClure, Whittle, Eric Fairclough, Henshall, Townroe, Skinner and Seymour Jones, Second Lieutenants Robertson, Frost and Dickson. . . .</p>
        <p>In addition there are Captain and Adjutant Izod, Lieutenant and Quartermaster Ingram, and Sergeant-Major Adams. Great enthusiasm prevails through the ranks.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>ARMY SERVICE CORPS.</head>
        <p>THE ARMY SERVICE CORPS.</p>
        <p>The local Company of the A.S.C. now totals but a few short of one hundred. Several squads have been drafted away during the week. The following are the officers :—Capts. Picton and Roberts, and Lieuts. 11. Fairclougk and Richard- son.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>MAYOR'S VIEW.</head>
        <p>THE MAYOR'S VIEW.</p>
        <p>Appeal for Level-headedness.</p>
        <p>" Examiner" Interview.</p>
        <p>Interviewed by "The Examiner" represen- tative yesterday, his Worship the Mayor said "It is a disastrous war, but, fortunately, it comes at a time when we are well prepared for it. - My opinion is, that if Englishmen will maintain the tradition of meeting a difficulty as they ought to do, we shall come out all right." After speaking of the very serious difficulties which would arise from people stocking in a lot of provisions, the Mayor said "When I came back here I found some of my friends ordering very heavily, and I told them it was the worst thing they could do; that they were forcing prices up, and eventually that it would recoil on their own heads, and be no good to them. ,In one or two businesses where I have control in regard to food I have prohibited them selling more than the usual quantities. People should not buy more than they need, and if prices go up, as they have, my advice to the middle man is to put up with it as long as he can and keep on buying as if nothing had happened. If the people are short of wages, then let them spend what they have got, and, if the price of goods does increase, to buy less. They should not restrict their spending powers at all."</p>
        <p>. GERMANS IN WARRINGTON. Not to be Offended. "There has, I understand, been a slight feel- ing in Warrington against Germans who live here. I am very sorry to find that in Warring- ton we have people who are inclined to be offensive to foreigners, and I think this shows very bad for the level-headedness of Warring- tonians. At all event, whatever the Germans do to their enemies, we ought to keep calm and not wilfully offend foreigners who have had no direct part in the dispute."</p>
        <p>PREPARATION. Machinery To Be Set Up. "We are having a meeting on Monday, at which there will be representatives of all classes. We are going to get the machipery ready to deal with any emergency at once, as regards the wives and children of those em- ployes who are going away. All employers can- not be termed 'model,' but we shall make all arrangements on Monday."</p>
        <p>I THE PUBLIC-HOUSE. , Should Not Be Closed, Says the Mayor. , In regard to the proposal that public-houses ' should be closed earlier, the Mayor said: I should be against closing public-houses in. , Warrington, because I think it would cause such a lot of friction amongst a trade which ,' conducts its business fairly well in difficult</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>WARRINGTON'S FOOD SUPPLY.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON'S FOOD SUPPLY.</p>
        <p>An Exciting Week Ends More Calmly and %Vith Prices Lower.</p>
        <p>NO BUSH ON BANKS.</p>
        <p>"Keep cool," said Britain's Prime Minister on Thursday. The people of Warrington have taken the advice to heart, and yesterday, although there was a vast amount ,of anxiety and plenty of war talk, the people did " keep cool." They obeyed to the letter the Premier's advice, and followed out the dictates of a phrase which will go down in history like Disraeli's famous "Peace with honour." The excitement which arose on the declaration of war had subsided by yesterday, with, perhaps, an exception in the case where reservists made their departure. In business circles a much more confident feeling was manifest, the workers also had got over the first dash of excitement, and generally a quiet confidence made itself felt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>NO BANK SCENES.</head>
        <p>NO BANK SCENES.</p>
        <p>Yesterday it was expected that there might be some scenes in connection with the re-open- ing of the banks, but none occurred, the confi- dence of the public in the arrangements made by the Chancellor of the Exchequer was com- plete, and so far as Warrington was concerned there were absolutely no signs of a bank rush. " To-day," said a bank official at closing time to "The Examiner" representative, " every- thing has passed off in quite a normal manner. In fact, it has been quite a normal Friday. There has been no sign of a rush in any form beyond the usual Friday 'rush when cashiers call for wage money." Very little paper money had reached Warring- ton up to yesterday, and the banks were still awaiting their supply. Gold was fairly plenti- ful and silver and copper coins were there in abundance.</p>
        <p>At the Post Office a considerable amount of gold and silver was paid in for postal orders, which were issued free•of charge pending the introduction of the new currency scheme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>FOOD PRICES EASIER</head>
        <p>FOOD PRICES EASIER</p>
        <p>-- The opening of the banks and ,the restored confidence of the public had a distinct effect upon the -food prices. Articles which jumped up from 150 to 200 per cent. during the excite- ment of the early part of the week, last night showed a downward tendency. Sugar, for in- stance, soon after the declaration of war, rose to 6d. per lb., the highest it has been since the Franco-German War of 1870, and had by last night come down to 4d. per lb., with a tendency to drop still further. Butter remained about 2d. dearer, cheese was becoming normal. Flour has practically remained stationary. Although there has been an increase in food stuffs, the feeling of shopkeepers yesterday was that very little more advance would be made. "If the British Fleet can bottle up the Ger- man Fleet," said one to "The Examiner :I' repre- sentative, "there should be no further Increase in prices. We are, told that we have four months' supply of wheat in hand or ready to be sent over the Atlantic, and consequently the cost of flour should not be much above the normal. Sugar is, perhaps, the most likely to be affected, as the supply comes from three of the Continental Powers involved. I see no occasion for alarm."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>RUSH ON THE SHOPS.</head>
        <p>RUSH ON THE SHOPS.</p>
        <p>Remarkable scenes were witnessed on Wednes- day afternoon in some of the Warrington shops. The war fever was then at its height, and the womenfolk were anxious to lay in a full cup- board of supplies. They 6rowiled into town and the principal shops which deal in food stuffs became so crowded that in some cases they had to close earlier than usual. _</p>
        <p>"We simply could not Supply the demand," was the cryptic way with which one shopkeeper explained. "Half a dozen sacks of flour were asked for by one customer. She got one. Now the rush is passing." The bakers, grocers and butchers seem to have felt the effect most. Butchers' meat naturally will be considerably affected by the war, but, according to one dealer, the supply will be nearly equal to the demand for some weeks to come, and by that time the Argentine, Australia, New Zealand and Chicago will be rushing in supplies. Generally, the view last night was that with Government assistance the food supply for the .people is assured.</p>
        <p>MOTOR DRAYS COMMANDEERED. Since Wednesday there has been, a continuous procession of motor drays through Warrington proceeding in all directions. Some had come as far away as Sheffield, and were supposed to be proceeding to Liverpool. Many locally owned motor and steam drays have been also com- mandeered, and the majority have been sent to Liverpool. The railways, as stated in another column, are already under the charge of the Governdent, and it is thought that the motor drays are to be largely used to distribute the people's food. As is pretty well known the railways will be re- quired for Army purposes, and the general</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <p>, --'I4ES •,•,*, , , fik4,40.,' ' Pe •", , . 4.• , , i';,,,,, , •,' ,'' , , , a ...9', 4, f z, , ,'', . Aiot ~ /' ''''' • " • f, • , 1 ~; „, zt.;'''' '//', /,/49&amp;%4 / '7/ 4. ' ' fl-'‘',,//fix,„ i, 5,,,,,,.., ~''' /01, ~ •0-01 ~,,i,",s-i,gl.. //,4/e,,----,,,2„,,ve „: „ ~v,,,,,, - . -.P. :•ki,14., ',13:4?-11-`.-,,,zi ',,,,,f, , , wiiv-,,,,,i.,, , , .:P.• ;ec..,,, ,'.• , .tre ', .-% 4-,,, g* ~,, •'' ' • resz:44•!: , , . • Zt'‘ii(t,',... ~,, ( 1A6e.V1.47,1?„,, :.,, ;;;I"''''''',,f ' - ' ~ „,...• ; , 44 i. '''' ' ... :</p>
        <p>Photograph by J. Smith.</p>
        <p>COLONEL FAIRCLOUGII, In command of the Warrington Territorials. Saw active service in South Africa.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>ACTION OF THE STORES.</head>
        <p>ACTION OF THE STORES.</p>
        <p>- - The Warrington Co-operative Stores have done much to allay the public excitement. They have offered to supply their members with food at the ordinary market prices as long as they can obtain supplies. The majority of the traders have also agreed to sell food at the lowest rates.</p>
        <p>The Co-operative Society have also informed their inembeks that their invested capital was perfectly safe, and they were asked not to with- draw it, but sums of not more than £5 would be paid at once, larger sums having to come under the consideration of the committee</p>
        <p>Photograph by Birtles. CAPTAIN APPLETON, Who Was in command of the Warrington Volun- teer Active Service Company in South Africa.</p>
        <p>The show at Walton Hall was held under die most depressing circumstances. It was the 25th in the history of the society, and never before Thursday was it held under such conditions. The weather was bad, the war upset everything, and the number of exhibits was not up to the average, while the attendance was said to be the poorest on record. The talk was all of " war," and farmers, usually keen on discussing cattle, horses and sheep, were speaking of nothing but the war. The stock was well up to the average of past years. This was remarkable, considering the difficAlty in conveying the exhibits. Nearly all were taken by road, the railways, now in the hands of the Army officials, were unable to carry stock, and this decidedly militated against the show. The jumping classes, always a feature at this show, suffered in consequence, but all the same some owners were plucky enough to send their prize jumpers from distances as far away as Yorkshire.</p>
        <p>„.•• • ••(.; •:,1•• ''''),7•l7''Ttz, • ion</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>ARMY OFFICIALS PRESENT.</head>
        <p>ARMY OFFICIALS PRESENT.</p>
        <p>In the light horse class there was a falling off in point of numbers. Some of the animals which should have been present had been comman- deered by the War Office. This certainly affected the show.</p>
        <p>Army officials were present to watch the horses on. show. They were in mufti, and did eery- thing to keep themselves under cover, but their presence was known and caused quite a flutter of excitement.</p>
        <p>Farmers spoken to about the matter said they did not know what was going to happen. In many cases they had left their wives at home, while they had come to the show, as they ex- pected a visit from Army officials to commandeer their horses.</p>
        <p>The crowd was nothing like what it would have been, and all through there was a note of depression. Even the band, the 6th Battalion of the Cheshire Regiment, deserted rag-time tunes for solemn music. The only bit of life in the show was supplied in the jumping, and at that there was the best attendance, though this did not nearly come up to the expectations of he promoters. SOME GOOD STOCK. According to the judges' reports the stock was particularly good. Once more heavy horses were a strong feature and the judges had a con- siderable amount of difficulty in giving their awards. Messrs. Peter Walker and Son made a capital exhibit, although they were not compet- ing to the usual extent. They sent three fine teams for exhibition only. One consisted of four horses, another consisted of a pair, and the other of a single-horsed dray, all the animals being well known prize takers. In the Shire horse classes there were many ! favourites of , past shows, and some fine young animals, which will become famous yet in the ring. The foals were described as particularly good. The Lymm stud did particularly well, ' Mr. James Gould again coming to the front with some fine animals. The Shires were described by experts as big, brawny animals, ranking amongst the best in the country.</p>
        <p>The light horkes were not so numerous as formerly, but what there were consisted of good stock. The judges had a difficult task on hand, but the decisions generally gave satisfaction. The hunters made a good show, the—animals all being above the average seen at the show for some years. Mr. Owen, of Northwich, had a very attractive animal, which brought honour to that stud. Mr. S. Raingill, Altrincham, was again a large winner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>CHAMPIONSHIPS.</head>
        <p>CHAMPIONSHIPS.</p>
        <p>The District Champion Cup was carried °off by "Delamere Combine," a bay filly, sired by "John Bull VII." This is the property of Mr. john W. Kenworthy, of Kelmll, near Chester, who has before taken championship honours at this show. The Shire Horse Society medal went to the same animal. The silver cup for the best Shire foal was secured by " Lymm Thumper," a very promising bay, of five months, which will yet make a name for itself in the ring. It is a sturdy animal of a fine build, and has been bred by Mr. James Gould.</p>
        <p>The silver cup for the best Shire exhibited by a tenant on the Walton estate was secured by a nine-year-old bay, owned by Mr. A. Broome, Windmill Farm, Preston Brook. The gold medal of the Hunters Improvement and National Light Horse Breeding Society for the best brood mare was won for Mr. Arthur Reiss, Winsford, by "Lady Flirt," an aged brown mare, at one time well known in the hunting field. The silver medal of the same society for .the best hunter filly was won by a chestnut yearling bred by Mr. J. G. Peel, Over Peover, near Knutsford. This is a fine built animal, and came in for much praise from experts. The Hackney Horse Society silver medal met with keen competition, and after careful judg- ing this went to a capital three-months-Old filly bred by Mr. John Makeague, of Golborne Park, Newton-le-Willows. In the cattle classes some useful animals were on view. The quality in the heifer classes showed a marked improvement upon past years, this indicating that local farmers are steadily improving the breeds. Bulls were a strong ex- hibit, and pigs were about the same in point of number and quality as-in the past. An attractive feature at the show is the jump- , ing contests. Unfortunately the entries were not so numerous, as owing to the difficulties of transit owners were somewhat chary about send- ing jumpers. Those that turned up, however, gave a capital exhibition.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>PRIZE LIST.</head>
        <p>PRIZE LIST.</p>
        <p>The following was the prize list SHIRES. District Classes.</p>
        <p>Novice Brood Mare, four years old and up- wards, with foal at foot, by a registered Shire stallion, not having won a first prize as a brood mare with foal at foot at any show prior to July 28th, 1914: 1, James Gould, Crouchley, Lymm; 2, John W. Kenworthy, Castle Hill, Kelsall; 3, Lewis Dodd, Park House, Rushton, Tarporley; 4, S. S. Raingill, The Grange, Ringway, Altrinc- ham; r, William Edge, Oak Tree Farm, Rush- ton, Tarporley ; he, William Blackshaw, Green- side Farm, Hatton. Brood Mare, four years old and upwards, with foal at foot: 1, James Gould, Crouchley ; 2, Dan Massey, Cruraleigh Heath, Little Leigh; 3, John Blackburn, Birkin Heath, Rostherue; 4, Wm. Blackshaw, Hatton. ' _ _ .</p>
        <p>Colt Foal, out of mare exhibited in Class 1 or 2: 1, James Gould, 'Crouchley; 2, John W. Kenworthy, Kelsall; 3, John Blackburn, 13irkin Heath, Rostherne; 4, .1. H. Jones, Lilac Farm, High Legh; r, Sam S. Raingill, Ringway; he, William Edge, Oak Tree Farm, Rushton, Tar- porley. Filly Foal, out of a mare exhibited in Class 1 or 2: 1 and 3, Robert Wade, Aston Grange; 2, Lewis Dodd, Tarporley; 4, Alfred Broome, Windmill Farm, Preston Brook; r and he, Will. Blackshaw, Hatton. Yearling Gelding or.. Filly : 1 and r, John W. Kenworthy, Kelsall; 2, Dan Massey, Little Leigh; 3, James Gould, Crouehley; he, Alfred Broome, Preston Brook; c, Thomas Smith, Union Bank, Bold. Two-year-old Gelding or Filly: 1, D. Massey, Little Leigh; 2, James Gould, Crouchley. Three-year-old' Gelding or Filly: .1, Samuel Jones and Sons, Poole Hall, Little Sutton; 2, D. Massey, Little Leigh; 3, William Leech, °leave House Farm, Mobberley..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>HUNTERS.</head>
        <p>HUNTERS.</p>
        <p>Open to Residents in the Counties of Lancaster and Chester. Brood Mare, having produced a living foal by, •or in foal to, a thoroughbred or 'registered honte.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-08-08_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>Open Classes.</head>
        <p>Open Classes.</p>
