        <p>WHAT Tiff WORKS IRE DOING.' LOCAL INTELLIGENCE. Commendable Assistance to be Given by the Employers. Many firms have made arrangements to see that the dependants of the Reserves and Terri- torials are catered for. Amongst these are the following, whose statements we give Messrs. Joseph Crosfield &amp; Sons, Ltd. Half-wages are to be paid to the wives of employes who have been called away. The minimum will be 10s., with is. extra for each child under 14 years of age. All time served with the Army or Navy will be counted as time served with the company in connection with the long service pay. A Special Committee has been organised to watch over the interests of the workers' families. Mers. Whitecross Co., Ltd, No definite arrangements yet made. Warrington Corporation. The matter will come before the General Pur- poses Committee on Monday. We understand there is no reason to anticipate that Warring- ton will treat its employes less generously than other towns. The Richmond Gas Stove &amp; Meter Co., Ltd. The works have been closed this week for the yearly holiday, and will re-open on Monday. Nothing, so far as we can ascertain, has been undertaken, but without doubt provision will be made for those in need. . St. Helens Cable Co., Ltd. Only a few men at these works have been called upon, but the dependents of those work,- men who have will be amply cared for. Work will be plentiful, War Office contracts being executed. Messrs. Rylands Bros., Ltd., Are to pay 10s. per week to the wives of work- men who have been regularly working with the firm, and who have been called upon to join the colours. Messrs. Pearson &amp; Knowles Coal and Iron Co., Ltd. A weekly payment of 10s. will be made to the wives of regular workmen who have been called away. Messrs. Robinson, Son &amp; Co., Ltd. No definite arrangements as yet. Warrington E.I. Co-operative Society. With the approval of the committee half- wages will be paid. British Aluminium Co., Ltd. The wives of employes called out will receive 10s. weekly. Messrs. Fletcher, Russell &amp; Co., Ltd. Matter held over pending meeting of employers. Castle Rubber Co., Ltd. Undoubtedly the firm will follow the, lead. Only a few men have been called away. Messrs. Monks, Hall &amp; Co., Ltd. No definite arrangements made as yet. Mersey White Lead Co., Ltd. • No definite arrangements made as yet. Messrs. N. Greening &amp; Sons, Ltd. No definite arrangements made as yet. Messrs. Peter Walker &amp; Son, Ltd. The firm will be all too pleased to help the dependents. Fifty men from Warrington have been called away. Messrs. Greenall, Whitley &amp; Co. Ltd. This week full wages will be paid for the married men called up for service, and the mat- ter will be re-considered for next week.</p>
        <p>Lpp, 1101 is °f Britain Forced by Germany to Fight. !no atot „cit. ,iiity ndof .1, 15 °,ll</p>
        <p>OUR CLEAR CONSCIENCE. Prime Minister and Germany's Infamous Proposal. INVITED TO DISHONOUR OURSELVES. The Prime Minister moved for a vote of credit of £100,000,000 for the purposes of the war in the Commons on Thursday. In doing so he de- livered one of the most weighty and impressive spi‘eches ever heard in the House. In proof of the contention that the war was forced upon this country, Mr. Asquith dealt with the diplomatic incidents immediately pre- ceding the rupture, quoting pregnant passages from despatches. Germany proposed to secure the neutrality of Britain by promising not to annex French territory. But this understand- ing she would not extend to the French colonies. As regards Belgium, Germany, while promis- ing integrity after the war was over, declined to</p>
        <p>night issued the following : H.M.S. Amphion sunk this morning after striking a mine. Paymaster C. D. Gedge and 130 men lost; captain and 16 officers and 135 men saved. The Secretary of the Admiralty issued the fol- lowing statement on Friday morning with re- gard to the loss of the Amphion In the course of reconnoitring after the mine- layer Koenigin Luise was sunk this morning the Amphion struck a mine and foundered. The fore part of the ship was shattered by the ex- plosion, and practically all the loss to the crew ensued from this cause. All not killed by the explosion were taken off by the destroyer's boats before she sank. Paymaster Gedge and over one hundred men were killed. The captain, 16 officers, and 135 men were saved. Twenty German prisoners of war who were confined in the forepart of the ship were killed in addition. ENGLAND'S STRONG POSITION. Plenty of Money and Plenty of Food. Our London correspondent, telegraphing last night, says:— The appointment of the Prince of Wales a second lieutenant in the Grenadier Guards, dated Bth August, is gazetted to-night. In the House of Commons Mr. Lloyd George announced that the Bank rate would be reduced to 5 per cent. to-morrow. The Bank of England received £5,600,000 in gold. Most banks reported more money had been received than was paid out. The Italian Ambassador in London, acknow- ledging the friendly demonstration outside the Embassy, declared that Italy would adhere to her .neutrality. The Board of Agriculture has announced that five months' supply of food stuffs is actually in this country, in addition to wheat and flour on passage.</p>
